LESSONS[23]={
  phase:'Phase 3 — Video Ads',title:'Day 23: Fashion Video Ad Campaign',
  tools:['Kling 2.6','Runway Gen-4.5'],time:'65 min',tags:['fashion','video','campaign','styling'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Fashion Video Ad — AI Campaign Production 2025',channel:'Theoretically Media',duration:'24:10',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fashion+video+ad+AI+kling+runway+campaign+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1a2e,#e91e63)'},
    {title:'Kling 2.6 Fashion & Model Physics Masterclass',channel:'AI Advantage',duration:'19:30',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=kling+fashion+model+fabric+physics+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#0f0c29,#302b63)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Pinterest: High Fashion Video Ads',desc:'Chanel, Dior, Valentino campaign videos. Study camera movement, model pacing, and atmosphere.',type:'pinterest',url:'https://pinterest.com/search/pins/?q=chanel+dior+fashion+campaign+video+cinematic',tag:'Reference'},
    {name:'Vogue: Best Fashion Campaigns',desc:'The highest standard in fashion campaign direction. Study pacing, light quality, and model direction.',type:'website',url:'https://www.vogue.com/fashion/campaigns',tag:'Benchmark'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#8b5cf622;color:#8b5cf6">👗</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Fashion Video Ads Have a Specific Grammar</div><div class="concept-card-sub">Pacing, light, fabric, and model direction — the 4 pillars</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>A fashion ad is not a product demo. It sells a <strong>feeling and identity</strong>, not a garment. The best fashion campaign videos rarely show the clothing clearly — they show aspiration, mood, and a world you want to belong to. Your prompts need to describe that world, not just the outfit.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#f3e8ff;border-color:#a855f7"><span class="hb-icon">🎬</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The 4 Pillars of a Fashion Video Prompt</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>1. Model direction:</strong> How is she/he moving? Confident stride, turning to camera, adjusting collar, looking away. Specificity wins.<br><strong>2. Fabric behavior:</strong> The clothing IS the hero. Describe material physics — silk, wool, leather each move differently.<br><strong>3. Camera storytelling:</strong> Slow tracking, push-in, low angle — the camera attitude defines the brand attitude.<br><strong>4. World-building:</strong> The environment is never neutral. Paris cobblestones vs Tokyo neon vs Sahara dunes each create a completely different brand identity.</div></div></div><div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// FASHION AD — LUXURY BRAND (KLING 2.6)</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this,\'Slow tracking shot following from the side. 26-year-old French model in oversized double-breasted cream wool coat, walking confidently along rain-wet Paris cobblestone street at blue hour twilight, coat lapels move gently with each stride, leather belt cinches waist, she glances briefly at camera then looks forward again. Neon brasserie sign casts warm amber spill on wet stones. Tracking camera maintains distance, stays at mid-body height. Chanel Coco Mademoiselle campaign aesthetic. 10s 16:9.\')">📋 Copy</button></div><div class="prompt-card-body">Slow tracking shot following from the side. 26-year-old French model in oversized double-breasted cream wool coat, walking confidently along rain-wet Paris cobblestone street at blue hour twilight, coat lapels move gently with each stride, leather belt cinches waist, she glances briefly at camera then looks forward again. Neon brasserie sign casts warm amber spill on wet stones. Tracking camera maintains distance, stays at mid-body height. Chanel Coco Mademoiselle campaign aesthetic. 10s 16:9.</div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#f59e0b22;color:#f59e0b">🧵</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Fabric Physics by Material — The Vocabulary</div><div class="concept-card-sub">Tell Kling WHAT the material is — it simulates it differently</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Kling 2.6 understands material physics. The key is naming the fabric explicitly. "Clothing" gives you generic results. "Floor-length bias-cut silk charmeuse" gives you liquid-like drape with light catching every fold.</p><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#8b5cf6">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Silk / Charmeuse / Satin</div><div class="step-desc">Prompt: "liquid drape of bias-cut silk, fabric catches and releases light as she moves, whisper-thin fabric responds to every micro-movement." Kling simulates the slip and weight beautifully.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#8b5cf6">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Wool / Overcoat / Cashmere</div><div class="step-desc">Prompt: "heavy wool overcoat swings with natural weight and momentum, structured shoulders, fabric falls back into position after each step." Heavier physics = different endpoint.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#8b5cf6">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Leather / Denim</div><div class="step-desc">Prompt: "butter-soft black leather jacket, minimal movement — material holds structure, subtle crease at elbow as she extends arm." Leather barely moves — and that rigidity is the luxury signal.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#8b5cf6">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Sheer / Organza / Tulle</div><div class="step-desc">Prompt: "floor-length ivory organza skirt, volume expands and contracts with walking rhythm, sheer layers catch backlight creating luminous halo effect, fabric settles with each pause."</div></div></li></ul></div></div><div class="compare-section"><div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:800;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:10px;">Fashion Ad: Beginner vs Expert Prompt</div><div class="compare-toggle-bar"><button class="compare-tab bad active" id="cmpd23-bad" onclick="switchCompare(\'cmpd23\',\'bad\')">❌ Beginner</button><button class="compare-tab good" id="cmpd23-good" onclick="switchCompare(\'cmpd23\',\'good\')">✅ Expert</button></div><div class="compare-panel bad-panel active" id="cmpd23-bad-panel"><div class="compare-prompt">"A model walking in a fashion ad wearing nice clothes"</div><div class="compare-annotation">No camera movement. No fabric type. No environment. No model direction. No brand reference. Kling will generate something completely generic and unusable.</div></div><div class="compare-panel good-panel" id="cmpd23-good-panel"><div class="compare-prompt">"Low angle push-in as model walks toward camera. 24-year-old South Asian model in floor-length bias-cut crimson silk gown, fabric drapes and catches studio light with each slow deliberate step on polished obsidian runway floor, long silver earrings sway gently, she maintains direct eye contact with camera, confident unhurried pace. Single overhead rim light creates halo, deep shadow fills sides. Valentino couture aesthetic. Camera settles as she stops 2m away. 10s 16:9."</div><div class="compare-annotation">Named camera move first. Specified exact fabric and color. Described physics behavior. Named the jewelry for secondary motion. Added model direction (eye contact, pacing). Environment defined (obsidian floor). Brand reference. Camera endpoint. This is a client-ready prompt.</div></div></div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 23: Fashion Video — 2 Brand Worlds',emoji:'👗',desc:'Create 2 fashion video prompts for opposite brand aesthetics — luxury editorial vs streetwear energy. Same model, completely different worlds.',deliverables:['Luxury/editorial prompt: slow camera, neutral palette, fine materials','Streetwear/energy prompt: fast camera, saturated colors, urban kinetics','List 5 specific words that changed between the two versions','Which world would you pitch first to a real fashion client — and why?'],placeholder:'Luxury prompt:\n\nStreetwear prompt:\n\n5 words that changed:\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n5.\n\nI\'d pitch [luxury/streetwear] first because:',systemPrompt:'You are a fashion video creative director. Review the student\'s 2-world contrast exercise. Do the prompts actually create opposite visual languages, or do they just swap one or two words? Identify the most powerful contrast element they used. Max 220 words.'}
};

LESSONS[24]={
  phase:'Phase 3 — Video Ads',title:'Day 24: E-Commerce Product Launch Video',
  tools:['Kling 2.6','Runway Gen-4.5'],time:'60 min',tags:['ecommerce','product','launch','commercial'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Product Launch Video AI — Commercial Workflow 2025',channel:'Theoretically Media',duration:'22:40',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=product+launch+video+AI+kling+runway+commercial+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#f7971e,#ffd200)'},
    {title:'E-Commerce Video Ads That Convert — AI Production',channel:'AI Advantage',duration:'18:20',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=ecommerce+video+ads+convert+AI+production+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#11998e,#38ef7d)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Pinterest: Product Launch Videos',desc:'High-converting product launch video references — study the reveal moment, physics, and CTA placement.',type:'pinterest',url:'https://pinterest.com/search/pins/?q=product+launch+video+cinematic+commercial+2026',tag:'Reference'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#f59e0b22;color:#f59e0b">📦</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">The Product Launch Video Has 3 Structural Acts</div><div class="concept-card-sub">Reveal → Hero → CTA — every commercial follows this arc</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Unlike a fashion ad (which sells a feeling), a product launch video must do a specific job: <strong>make the product desirable AND clearly identifiable</strong>. This requires a 3-act structure even in a 10-second clip. Miss any act and the ad fails its commercial purpose.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#fef9e7;border-color:#f59e0b"><span class="hb-icon">📐</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The 3-Act Product Video Formula</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>Act 1 — The Reveal (0–3s):</strong> Product emerges from or is introduced into the frame. Dramatic entry — rising from surface, camera pulling back to reveal, or product dropping into scene with impact. This creates the first dopamine hit.<br><strong>Act 2 — The Hero Shot (3–8s):</strong> Product is the undeniable star. Orbit, close-up detail, material texture visible. Best feature shown in context.<br><strong>Act 3 — The Rest (8–10s):</strong> Camera settles or pulls back to show product in aspirational context. Brand world established.</div></div></div><div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// PRODUCT LAUNCH — SKINCARE SERUM (KLING 2.6)</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this,\'Act 1: Camera starts tight on dark wet marble surface, premium amber glass serum bottle slowly rises from below frame with smooth mechanical precision, water droplets scatter as it emerges. Act 2: Camera transitions to slow orbital arc around the bottle, golden serum liquid visible through glass, light refracts through amber creating internal glow, condensation on glass surface. Act 3: Camera settles at eye level, minimalist white background. Single overhead rim light throughout. La Mer campaign quality. Bottle comes to rest perfectly still. 10s 16:9.\')">📋 Copy</button></div><div class="prompt-card-body">Act 1: Camera starts tight on dark wet marble surface, premium amber glass serum bottle slowly rises from below frame with smooth mechanical precision, water droplets scatter as it emerges. Act 2: Camera transitions to slow orbital arc around the bottle, golden serum liquid visible through glass, light refracts through amber creating internal glow, condensation on glass surface. Act 3: Camera settles at eye level, minimalist white background. Single overhead rim light throughout. La Mer campaign quality. Bottle comes to rest perfectly still. 10s 16:9.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#10b98122;color:#10b981">💡</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">The Reveal Mechanic — 5 Ways to Enter the Frame</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The reveal moment is worth 80% of your prompt attention</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>How the product enters the frame determines whether the viewer keeps watching. A boring static reveal loses attention in 1.5 seconds. A compelling reveal creates curiosity and earns the next 8 seconds of attention.</p><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Rise from Surface</div><div class="step-desc">Product rises from sand, water, marble, fabric. Prompt: "product emerges slowly from [surface], rising with smooth mechanical precision, [material] particles disturbed by its emergence settling around base."</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Camera Pull-Back Reveal</div><div class="step-desc">Start in extreme close-up of product detail, then camera slowly pulls back to reveal the full product. Creates "oh that\'s what I was looking at" dopamine. Prompt: "camera starts on extreme macro of [product detail], slowly pulls back revealing full [product] in [context]."</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Material Impact Drop</div><div class="step-desc">Product drops into liquid, sand, or powder with impact physics. Prompt: "product drops onto [surface] from above, impact creates [material] splash/scatter with realistic physics, product settles perfectly upright."</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Atmospheric Emergence</div><div class="step-desc">Product appears through mist, smoke, or light. Prompt: "volumetric mist parts to reveal [product] in dramatic single shaft of light, product already in position as atmosphere clears."</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">5</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Hands — Human Introduction</div><div class="step-desc">Product handed into frame by elegant hands. Creates trust and human scale. Prompt: "elegant female hands with natural nail color carry [product] into frame from below, place it precisely on [surface]."</div></div></li></ul></div></div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 24: Product Launch — 3-Act Video',emoji:'📦',desc:'Choose a product. Write a 3-act launch video prompt that builds desire from setup to reveal to CTA. Make the reveal moment unmissable.',deliverables:['Product + launch objective (awareness vs conversion)','Act 1 prompt: the problem or desire — no product yet','Act 2 prompt: the reveal moment — dramatic, specific','Act 3 prompt: product in use — lifestyle, aspiration, brand world'],placeholder:'Product:\nObjective:\n\nAct 1 (desire/problem):\n\nAct 2 (reveal):\n\nAct 3 (lifestyle):\n\nReveal technique I used:\nWhy this sequence works:',systemPrompt:'You are a product launch video director. Review the student\'s 3-act prompt structure. Does the tension build properly? Is the reveal moment genuinely dramatic or just descriptive? Give specific feedback on making Act 2 more cinematic and memorable. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[25]={
  phase:'Phase 3 — Video Ads',title:'Day 25: Google Flow & Veo 3.1 Mastery — Structured Scenes & Audio',
  tools:['Google Flow','Kling 2.6'],time:'65 min',tags:['veo','structured','audio','google','cinematic'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Google Veo 3 — Complete Guide to Prompting & Audio',channel:'Theoretically Media',duration:'26:10',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=google+veo+3+complete+guide+prompting+audio+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#1a73e8,#34a853)'},
    {title:'Veo 3 vs Kling vs Runway — Which Wins for Commercial Work?',channel:'AI Advantage',duration:'22:40',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=veo+3+vs+kling+vs+runway+commercial+comparison+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#0f0c29,#302b63)'},
    {title:'AI Video with Synchronized Audio — Veo 3 AudioGen',channel:'Matt Wolfe',duration:'18:30',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=veo+3+audio+generation+synchronized+sound+design+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#11998e,#38ef7d)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Google VideoFX — Veo Showcase',desc:'Google\'s own Veo showcase. Study the structured, realistic outputs and how audio is integrated.',type:'website',url:'https://labs.google/fx/tools/video-fx',tag:'Reference'},
    {name:'Pinterest: Luxury Cinematic Video',desc:'The visual benchmark for structured luxury video — where Veo excels most.',type:'pinterest',url:'https://pinterest.com/search/pins/?q=luxury+cinematic+video+ad+structured+2026',tag:'Mood Board'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#1a73e822;color:#1a73e8">🌊</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Veo 3.1 — The Rendering Engine Mental Model</div><div class="concept-card-sub">Think in structured ingredients, not cinematic language</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Kling is a choreographer — you describe a performance. Runway is a physicist — you describe forces. <strong>Veo 3.1 is a rendering engine — you give it structured, precise specifications and it executes them with exceptional environmental realism.</strong> The prompt style that works best for Veo is closer to a technical brief than a creative description. Each element defined separately. No ambiguity. No poetic language. Ingredient list, not story.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#e8f0fe;border-color:#1a73e8"><span class="hb-icon">🎯</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">Veo 3.1 vs Kling 2.6 — When to Choose Which</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>Choose Veo 3.1 when:</strong><br>• You need synchronized natural audio (ambient sound, specific SFX)<br>• The scene is primarily environmental — architecture, landscape, structured interiors<br>• You need photorealistic people in realistic settings without exaggerated physics<br>• You want Nano Banana reference images to guide consistency<br>• The brief requires documentary or journalistic realism<br><br><strong>Choose Kling 2.6 when:</strong><br>• Material physics are the hero (fabric, liquid, atmospheric)<br>• You need longer clips (Veo max is shorter)<br>• You want native music generation alongside video<br>• Character consistency via Elements feature is needed</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#34a85322;color:#34a853">🎵</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Veo 3.1 Audio Generation — Unique to Google</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The only major video model that natively generates synchronized sound design</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Veo 3.1 generates <strong>synchronized audio alongside video</strong> — not just ambient background music, but physically accurate sound design. Footsteps on gravel sound like gravel. Waves sound like the specific ocean in the frame. A wine glass being placed on marble has the exact acoustic signature of glass on stone. This is a completely different capability from Kling\'s ambient audio.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#e6f4ea;border-color:#34a853"><span class="hb-icon">🔊</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">How to Prompt Veo Audio</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>Direct audio instruction:</strong> Add "Audio: [description]" as a separate line in your Veo prompt.<br><br><strong>Example prompts:</strong><br>"Audio: quiet ambient kitchen sounds, the gentle pour of liquid, no music"<br>"Audio: city street ambience, distant traffic, wind between buildings, footsteps on stone"<br>"Audio: total silence except for the sound of [specific object]"<br>"Audio: luxury spa ambient, soft water feature, no dialogue, no music"<br><br><strong>Rule:</strong> Specify absence as clearly as presence. "No music" is as important as describing what you want to hear.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#f59e0b22;color:#f59e0b">📋</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">The Veo Structured Prompt Format</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The ingredient-list method — completely different from Kling and Runway</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Veo responds best to prompts structured as labeled sections. Think of it as briefing a rendering department — every specification on its own line, clearly labeled. This is the opposite of the narrative-style prompts that work for Kling.</p><div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// VEO 3.1 — LUXURY PRODUCT AD (STRUCTURED FORMAT)</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this,&quot;Scene: A single premium whisky bottle on a hand-cut crystal surface in a dark private members club.\nCamera: Slow push-in from 1 metre to 30cm, ending on the liquid level visible through the glass.\nLighting: Single warm tungsten source from upper right, deep shadow left, amber specular on bottle shoulder.\nEnvironment: Dark mahogany panelling, out-of-focus leather seating at edges, cigar smoke through background light beam.\nSubject behaviour: Bottle completely still. Smoke drifts slowly. No other movement.\nDuration: 8 seconds.\nStyle: Johnnie Walker Blue Label campaign. Film quality.\nAudio: Quiet ambient room. Faint ice settling off frame. No music.&quot;)">📋 Copy</button></div><div class="prompt-card-body">Scene: A single premium whisky bottle on a hand-cut crystal surface in a dark private members club.<br>Camera: Slow push-in from 1 metre to 30cm, ending on the liquid level visible through the glass.<br>Lighting: Single warm tungsten source from upper right, deep shadow left, amber specular on bottle shoulder.<br>Environment: Dark mahogany panelling, out-of-focus leather seating, cigar smoke through background light beam.<br>Subject behaviour: Bottle completely still. Smoke drifts slowly. No other movement.<br>Duration: 8 seconds.<br>Style: Johnnie Walker Blue campaign. Film quality.<br>Audio: Quiet ambient room. Faint ice settling off frame. No music.</div></div><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#fef9e7;border-color:#f59e0b"><span class="hb-icon">⚡</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">Veo vs Kling — Same Brief, Different Prompt Style</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>Kling prompt for same scene:</strong> "Barely perceptible push-in. A single premium whisky bottle rests on cut crystal, warm amber tungsten light from upper right, 80% deep shadow, faint cigar smoke drifts through a background light beam, liquid visible through glass, complete stillness except the drift of smoke which then settles. Johnnie Walker Blue aesthetic. 8s 16:9."<br><br><strong>Veo prompt:</strong> Uses labeled sections (Scene / Camera / Lighting / Environment / Subject behaviour / Audio).<br><br>Same result, completely different language. Veo needs structure. Kling needs description. Use both prompts — compare which gives better output for your specific scene type.</div></div></div></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon green">🔧</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">Tool Spotlight: Google Flow — Deep Dive</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">AI filmmaking combining Veo 3.1, Nano Banana, and Gemini</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">📹</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Scene Builder</div><div class="step-desc">Compose cinematic scenes with camera control, object insertion, video extension. Natural language.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">🖼</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Image-to-Video</div><div class="step-desc">Generate image with Nano Banana, animate with Veo 3.1 — all in one workspace.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">🎧</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Native Audio</div><div class="step-desc">Veo 3.1 generates environmental sounds and dialogue natively — audio built into the video.</div></div></li> </ul> <p><em>Free tier: 50 credits/day. <a href="https://labs.google/fx/tools/flow" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--primary);font-weight:700;">Open Google Flow →</a></em></p> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 25: Veo 3.1 — Scene + Audio Design',emoji:'🌊',desc:'Write 2 Veo-format prompts where audio is a critical story element. Audio isn\'t background — it IS the scene.',deliverables:['Scene 1: environment where ambient sound tells the story (write audio description too)','Scene 2: product moment where sound = desire (e.g. coffee pour, crunch, fizz)','Compare one Veo prompt against an equivalent Kling prompt — what\'s different?','What can Veo\'s audio generate that pure visual prompts can\'t do?'],placeholder:'Scene 1 prompt:\nAudio description:\nWhy audio matters here:\n\nScene 2 prompt:\nAudio description:\nWhy audio matters here:\n\nVeo vs Kling difference:\nWhat audio-native storytelling unlocks:',systemPrompt:'You are a Veo 3.1 specialist and audio-visual storytelling director. Review the student\'s audio-first prompts. Are they writing audio as a narrative element or just a technical afterthought? Give specific guidance on how to write richer audio cues in the Veo format. Max 230 words.'}
};


LESSONS[26]={
  phase:'Phase 3 — Video Ads',title:'Day 26: Fitness & Sports Brand Reel',
  tools:['Kling 2.6','Runway Gen-4.5'],time:'60 min',tags:['fitness','sports','energy','reel'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Fitness Brand Reel AI — Nike Adidas Energy Level',channel:'Theoretically Media',duration:'21:30',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fitness+brand+reel+AI+kling+runway+nike+adidas+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#0f0c29,#2575fc)'},
    {title:'High Energy Sports Video Prompts — Motion Language',channel:'AI Advantage',duration:'17:40',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=high+energy+sports+video+AI+motion+language+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#f7971e,#ffd200)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Pinterest: Nike & Adidas Campaign Videos',desc:'The benchmark for sports brand energy. Study how they use environment, light, and athlete body language.',type:'pinterest',url:'https://pinterest.com/search/pins/?q=nike+adidas+campaign+video+fitness+energy+2026',tag:'Benchmark'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#2563eb22;color:#2563eb">⚡</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Fitness Video is the Opposite Grammar to Luxury</div><div class="concept-card-sub">Energy, speed, impact, imperfection — these are the signals</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Where luxury whispers, fitness shouts. Where luxury uses one element, fitness uses environment, movement, sweat, environment chaos, and human determination together. The fundamental principle: <strong>the human body under exertion is the product</strong>. Clothes, shoes, and equipment are supporting characters.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#eff6ff;border-color:#2563eb"><span class="hb-icon">💪</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The Fitness Video Prompt Stack</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>1. Athlete action (first):</strong> Specific exercise with specific form — not "running" but "400m track sprinter exploding from starting blocks, full leg extension, forward lean, arms driving"<br><strong>2. Environment tension:</strong> The world pushes back — rain, track marks, weight room chalk, early morning mist, competitor in background<br><strong>3. Camera aggression:</strong> Low angle, tracking from ground level, handheld energy, Dutch angle for instability<br><strong>4. Physical evidence:</strong> Sweat, breath condensation, dirt, muscle tension, grimace — these are the truth signals of real effort<br><strong>5. The moment:</strong> Not the warm-up, not the finish line — the PEAK MOMENT of maximum effort</div></div></div><div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// FITNESS REEL — NIKE LEVEL (KLING 2.6)</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this,\'Low angle tracking shot from track surface level. Elite female sprinter exploding from starting blocks, full extension in first stride, track surface rushing past in foreground blur, arms driving powerfully, jaw set with determination, sweat visible on temples and collarbone, stadium lights create dramatic rim light on muscular form, competitor visible as motion blur in far lane, track lines converge toward vanishing point behind her. Handheld tracking camera with purposeful shake. Nike campaign. She reaches peak speed as camera loses pace behind her. 10s 16:9.\')">📋 Copy</button></div><div class="prompt-card-body">Low angle tracking shot from track surface level. Elite female sprinter exploding from starting blocks, full extension in first stride, track surface rushing past in foreground blur, arms driving powerfully, jaw set with determination, sweat visible on temples and collarbone, stadium lights create dramatic rim light on muscular form, competitor visible as motion blur in far lane, track lines converge toward vanishing point behind her. Handheld tracking camera with purposeful shake. Nike campaign. She reaches peak speed as camera loses pace behind her. 10s 16:9.</div></div></div></div></div> <div class="compare-section"> <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:800;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:10px;">🏋 Same Athlete — Two Brand Stories</div> <div class="compare-toggle-bar"> <button class="compare-tab bad active" id="cmp26-a" onclick="switchCompare(\'cmp26\',\'bad\')">Peak Moment</button> <button class="compare-tab good" id="cmp26-b" onclick="switchCompare(\'cmp26\',\'good\')">Recovery Moment</button> </div> <div class="compare-panel bad-panel active" id="cmp26-a-panel"> <div class="compare-prompt">Kling prompt: "Female powerlifter at maximum lift, bar overhead, face screaming effort, camera crashes upward from floor to overhead angle, strobe flash lighting on black background, white chalk dust erupting from hands in slow motion, aggressive handheld energy"</div> <div class="compare-annotation"><strong>Brand message: You can do the impossible.</strong> This is the aspirational peak. It sells the dream. Nike, Adidas use this to create desire. It works because it shows an extreme most viewers have never experienced.</div> </div> <div class="compare-panel good-panel" id="cmp26-b-panel"> <div class="compare-prompt">Kling prompt: "Same athlete, post-workout, seated on weight bench, head dropped, catching breath, golden afternoon light through gym window, slow dolly backward, hands on thighs, visible exhaustion and quiet satisfaction, Lululemon warm tone aesthetic"</div> <div class="compare-annotation"><strong>Brand message: Every rep is worth it.</strong> The recovery shot sells authenticity. It says we know how hard this is. Lululemon, Gymshark use this. It humanizes the brand. Together, peak + recovery tells the complete athlete story.</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon amber">⚡</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">Energy Language for Fitness Video</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">Communicate exertion level through camera and motion — not adjectives</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">🔥</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Maximum Exertion Language</div><div class="step-desc">Camera: shaky handheld, low ground-level angle, fast tracking. Motion: explosive, burst, propulsive, snap-cut rhythm. Lighting: harsh, high contrast, sweat visible. Prompt example: "athlete explodes upward, handheld camera crashes low tracking the jump, freeze frame at peak height, high contrast dramatic side lighting, sweat droplets frozen mid-air"</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">🌙</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Recovery / Reflection Language</div><div class="step-desc">Camera: slow dolly, eye-level. Motion: slowing, breathing visible, deliberate. Lighting: golden, warm, soft. Prompt example: "athlete seated on gym floor, head bowed, hands on knees, slow push-back camera, warm golden hour light through gym windows, dust particles in light shafts, quiet earned moment"</div></div></li> </ul> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 26: Fitness Reel — Peak + Recovery',emoji:'⚡',desc:'Create 2 contrasting fitness videos: maximum exertion and post-workout reflection. Same athlete, same brand — different emotional beats.',deliverables:['Fitness brand + athlete archetype','Peak exertion prompt: maximum effort, camera aggression, primal energy','Recovery prompt: quiet, earned, contemplative — the other side of performance','Which brands use this contrast well — and why does it work commercially?'],placeholder:'Brand:\nAthlete:\n\nPeak exertion prompt:\nCamera style:\nMotion language:\n\nRecovery prompt:\nCamera style:\nMood language:\n\nBrands I see doing this:\nWhy the contrast works:',systemPrompt:'You are a fitness brand creative director. Review the student\'s peak vs recovery contrast. Is the energy shift communicated through specific camera and motion language, or just mood words? Give feedback on making the contrast more visually striking and commercially compelling. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[27]={
  phase:'Phase 3 — Video Ads',title:'Day 27: AI Spokesperson & Ad Visuals with ChatGPT',
  tools:['ChatGPT Images','ElevenLabs','CapCut Pro'],time:'60 min',tags:['heygen','spokesperson','avatar','voiceover'],
  videos:[
    {title:'HeyGen Complete Tutorial — AI Spokesperson 2025',channel:'Matt Wolfe',duration:'26:40',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=heygen+complete+tutorial+AI+spokesperson+avatar+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#667eea,#764ba2)'},
    {title:'ElevenLabs + HeyGen — Full Ad Production Workflow',channel:'AI Advantage',duration:'20:10',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=elevenlabs+heygen+AI+ad+production+workflow+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#f953c6,#b91d73)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'HeyGen Template Gallery',desc:'Browse HeyGen\'s own template library to understand the range of spokesperson styles and background options.',type:'website',url:'https://www.heygen.com/templates',tag:'Reference'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#8b5cf622;color:#8b5cf6">🤖</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">HeyGen — What It Is and When to Use It</div><div class="concept-card-sub">AI spokespersons for explainers, ads, and multilingual scale</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>HeyGen creates photorealistic AI video presenters from a script. You choose an avatar (or create your own from a 2-minute video upload), write your script, and HeyGen generates a talking-head video with lip-sync and natural delivery. For agencies and brands, this eliminates talent fees, studio booking, and reshoot costs.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#f3e8ff;border-color:#a855f7"><span class="hb-icon">🎯</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">3 Commercial Use Cases for HeyGen</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>1. Product explainer ads:</strong> "Hi, I\'m Sarah, and I want to show you why this skincare routine changed my mornings." 60-second direct-response ad. Costs £0 in talent fees.<br><strong>2. Multilingual campaigns:</strong> Create one video in English, translate to 10 languages with HeyGen\'s translation feature. Same avatar, same gestures, different language. Market expansion for zero additional cost.<br><strong>3. Personalized outreach:</strong> Create 100 personalized "Hi [Name]" videos for sales teams using HeyGen\'s bulk generation. Each video feels personal.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#10b98122;color:#10b981">✍️</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Writing Scripts for AI Spokespersons</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The delivery is only as good as the script</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>HeyGen reads your script. The way you write it determines the delivery. Punctuation, sentence length, and pause markers all affect how the avatar sounds. Most beginners write long complex sentences — the avatar sounds robotic. Short sentences with deliberate pauses sound natural.</p><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Write Short Sentences</div><div class="step-desc">Bad: "This innovative skincare serum combines advanced retinol technology with hyaluronic acid to deliver transformative results." Good: "This serum has one job. Make your skin look 10 years younger. In 28 days." Short sentences create rhythm and emphasis.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Use SSML Pause Tags for Emphasis</div><div class="step-desc">HeyGen supports pause markers. Write [pause] or use ... for a natural beat. "This is the product... [pause] ...that changed everything." Strategic pauses create emphasis and feel human.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Front-Load the Hook</div><div class="step-desc">The first 3 seconds determine whether the viewer keeps watching. Start with a problem, a provocative claim, or a question. Never start with "Hi, my name is..." — that loses 60% of viewers before the second sentence.</div></div></li></ul><div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// HEYGEN SCRIPT — 30s SKINCARE AD</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this,\'Your skin is talking to you. The question is: are you listening? Most moisturisers sit on the surface. They feel good. For about 20 minutes. Then nothing. [pause] This one works differently. The technology goes deeper. Past the surface layer. To where real change happens. [pause] 28 days. That\'s all we ask. Because that\'s all it takes. [pause] Your skin. Transformed. Guaranteed.\')">📋 Copy</button></div><div class="prompt-card-body">Your skin is talking to you. The question is: are you listening? Most moisturisers sit on the surface. They feel good. For about 20 minutes. Then nothing. [pause] This one works differently. The technology goes deeper. Past the surface layer. To where real change happens. [pause] 28 days. That\'s all we ask. Because that\'s all it takes. [pause] Your skin. Transformed. Guaranteed.</div></div></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon blue">🤖</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">Writing Scripts for AI Spokespersons</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">AI avatars read differently than humans — write for the medium</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Short sentences only</div><div class="step-desc">AI avatars handle sentences under 15 words naturally. Longer sentences create unnatural pauses and awkward breathing. Write: "This serum changes everything. Clinically tested. Results in 7 days." Not: "This serum has been clinically tested and shown to deliver visible results in as few as seven days of consistent use."</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Use ... or [pause] for emphasis beats</div><div class="step-desc">HeyGen supports pause markers in scripts. "This is the product... [pause] ...that changed everything." Strategic pauses create emphasis and feel more human. Without them, the avatar reads at a constant pace with no rhythm.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Match avatar personality to brand voice</div><div class="step-desc">HeyGen offers dozens of avatars. Choose based on: industry (medical = professional, fitness = energetic), gender alignment with target audience, and language/accent for regional markets. A wellness brand should not use an aggressive salesperson avatar — the mismatch kills credibility.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">30 seconds = 75-80 words maximum</div><div class="step-desc">Average speaking pace for a natural delivery is 130-150 words per minute. At 30 seconds, that is 65-75 words. Write short. Then cut 20% more. Every word must earn its place in a spokesperson ad.</div></div></li> </ul> <div class="prompt-card"> <div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// 30-SECOND SCRIPT TEMPLATE — FILL IN THE BRACKETS</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this, \'Hook (3s): [One sentence that names the problem your audience has]. Problem (7s): [Expand — what does this cost them? Time, money, confidence?]. Solution (10s): [Your product enters.] [What it does in one sentence.] [Specific result or proof.]. CTA (5s): [Link in bio.] [Limited time.] [Start today.]. Total: max 75 words.\')">📋 Copy</button></div> <div class="prompt-card-body">Hook: <span class="prompt-param">[name the problem]</span>. Problem: <span class="prompt-param">[expand — what does it cost them?]</span>. Solution: <span class="prompt-style">[product enters] [result in one sentence]</span>. CTA: <span class="prompt-neg">[link] [urgency]</span>. Max 75 words total.</div> </div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon green">🔧</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">Tool Spotlight: ChatGPT Images for Ad Visuals</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">Conversational iteration is the fastest way to create ad concepts</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <p><strong>ChatGPT Images</strong> — Iterate ad visuals in conversation: "Create a professional person presenting a product" → "more approachable" → "add product in hand." Pair with <strong>ElevenLabs</strong> for voiceover and <strong>CapCut</strong> for editing. This 3-tool pipeline replaces expensive production. <a href="https://chatgpt.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--primary);font-weight:700;">Open ChatGPT →</a></p> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 27: HeyGen — Create Your Ad Avatar',emoji:'🤖',desc:'Set up a HeyGen avatar and generate a 15-second spokesperson clip for one product. Focus on avatar selection, script clarity, and brand voice match.',deliverables:['Product + brand voice (choose: warm / expert / energetic / authoritative)','Your 15-second script (max 35 words — tight and punchy)','Avatar chosen and why it matches the brand voice','Paste or describe the generated clip — what worked, what felt off?'],placeholder:'Product:\nBrand voice:\n\n15-second script (max 35 words):\n\nAvatar chosen:\nWhy it matches:\n\nClip result:\nWhat worked:\nWhat felt off:',systemPrompt:'You are a HeyGen specialist and video advertising director. Review the student\'s spokesperson setup. Is the script 35 words or under? Does the avatar choice match the brand voice logically? Give feedback on script tightness and how to direct an AI avatar for more natural delivery. Max 230 words.'}
};

