LESSONS[31]={
  phase:'Phase 4 — Brand Systems',title:'Day 31: Brand Identity Systems',
  tools:['Midjourney v8','Adobe Firefly'],time:'65 min',tags:['brand','identity','system','consistency'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Brand Identity System — Complete AI Workflow 2025',channel:'The Futur',duration:'38:40',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brand+identity+system+AI+workflow+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#2c3e50,#4ca1af)'},
    {title:'What Makes a Brand System — Not Just a Logo',channel:'Sagi Haviv',duration:'28:10',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brand+identity+system+principles+logo+design+sagi+haviv',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1a1a,#6c6c6c)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Brand New — Identity Reviews',desc:'The most respected brand identity critique platform. Study how professionals evaluate brand systems holistically.',type:'website',url:'https://www.underconsideration.com/brandnew/',tag:'Professional'},
    {name:'Pinterest: Brand Identity Systems',desc:'Full brand identity presentations — logo, color, typography, applications. Study the system, not just the logo.',type:'pinterest',url:'https://pinterest.com/search/pins/?q=brand+identity+system+presentation+2026',tag:'Reference'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#10b98122;color:#10b981">🎨</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">A Brand Identity is a System, Not a Logo</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The logo is 5% of the brand. The system is 95%.</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Most beginners think a brand = a logo. Professional brand designers know that a logo is just the trigger — the system is what actually creates consistent, recognizable brand experience. The system includes: color palette, typography hierarchy, photographic style, iconography style, tone of voice, spacing principles, and application rules. Miss any of these and the brand falls apart in the wild.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#d1fae5;border-color:#10b981"><span class="hb-icon">📐</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The 7 Components of a Complete Brand Identity</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>1. Logo system:</strong> Primary, secondary, icon-only versions. Minimum sizes. Clear space rules.<br><strong>2. Color palette:</strong> Primary (1–2 colors), secondary (2–4 colors), neutral (1–2 backgrounds). Each with HEX, RGB, CMYK, Pantone values.<br><strong>3. Typography:</strong> Display font (headlines), body font (reading), UI font (if digital). Max 2 typefaces — never 3.<br><strong>4. Photography style:</strong> Defined by 5 parameters: subject, light, palette, composition, mood.<br><strong>5. Iconography:</strong> Line weight, corner radius, fill vs stroke — all consistent.<br><strong>6. Grid & spacing:</strong> The invisible structure that makes everything feel coherent.<br><strong>7. Voice & tone:</strong> 3 adjectives that define how the brand speaks. Then: what it says, and what it never says.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#2563eb22;color:#2563eb">🔑</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Creating a Brand Photography Style Guide with AI</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The locked variables that create visual consistency at scale</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>The photography style guide is the most actionable output for an AI designer. It defines the exact prompt template that every photographer (AI or human) uses for every brand image. When done correctly, you can generate 50 brand images and they all feel like they came from the same campaign.</p><div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// BRAND PHOTOGRAPHY STYLE TEMPLATE</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this,\'BRAND: Minimal skincare brand targeting 28-35 year old professional women\nLOCKED VARIABLES (never change):\n- Light: Single directional window light from left, soft diffused\n- Palette: Warm cream, sage green, warm white — never cool tones\n- Lens: 85mm f/1.4, shallow depth of field\n- Surface: Raw linen, white marble, or warm wood only\n- Style: Kinfolk magazine, Scandinavian minimal, authentic\nVARIABLE SLOTS:\n- [PRODUCT]: Swap per image\n- [CONTEXT]: Vary — bathroom shelf, bedside table, morning window\n- [SEASON]: Winter morning / summer afternoon\nEXAMPLE PROMPT: [PRODUCT] on [SURFACE], [CONTEXT], single directional window light from left soft diffused, 85mm lens shallow depth, warm cream and sage palette, Kinfolk minimal aesthetic\')">📋 Copy</button></div><div class="prompt-card-body">BRAND: Minimal skincare brand targeting 28–35 year old professional women<br>LOCKED VARIABLES: Single left window light · warm cream/sage palette · 85mm lens · linen/marble/wood surface · Kinfolk style<br>VARIABLE SLOTS: [PRODUCT] · [CONTEXT] · [SEASON]</div></div></div></div> <div class="compare-section"> <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:800;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:10px;">🔍 Locked System vs No System</div> <div class="compare-toggle-bar"> <button class="compare-tab bad active" id="cmp31-bad" onclick="switchCompare(\'cmp31\',\'bad\')">❌ No System</button> <button class="compare-tab good" id="cmp31-good" onclick="switchCompare(\'cmp31\',\'good\')">✅ Locked System</button> </div> <div class="compare-panel bad-panel active" id="cmp31-bad-panel"> <div class="compare-prompt">Image 1: golden hour warm tones, 50mm / Image 2: cool blue studio light, 85mm / Image 3: overcast flat light, 35mm wide</div> <div class="compare-annotation"><strong>This is what most beginners produce.</strong> Each image looks good individually. Together they look like 3 different brands. No client would approve this as a campaign — it shows no system, no brand thinking, no professional control.</div> </div> <div class="compare-panel good-panel" id="cmp31-good-panel"> <div class="compare-prompt">All 3 images: warm golden hour tones ✓, 50mm lens always ✓, f/1.8 shallow depth ✓, subject in left third ✓, linen texture visible ✓, Kinfolk aesthetic ✓</div> <div class="compare-annotation"><strong>This is what a brand system produces.</strong> Every locked variable is consistent. Subject and action change — everything else stays identical. A client can add 20 more images in 6 months and they will still look like one coherent brand. This is what you are selling.</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon blue">📋</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">Brand System Master Template</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">Fill in the brackets — deliver this to every client</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <div class="prompt-card"> <div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// BRAND SYSTEM MASTER TEMPLATE</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this, \'[SUBJECT: specific person or product description], [ACTION: what they do in this image], [ENVIRONMENT: specific location], [LOCKED PALETTE] color palette, [LOCKED LIGHT: direction and quality], [LOCKED LENS and aperture], [LOCKED STYLE REFERENCE], --ar [LOCKED RATIO] --no [LOCKED EXCLUSIONS]\')">📋 Copy</button></div> <div class="prompt-card-body"><span class="prompt-param">[SUBJECT]</span> + <span class="prompt-param">[ACTION]</span> + [ENVIRONMENT], <span class="prompt-style">[LOCKED PALETTE], [LOCKED LIGHT], [LOCKED LENS], [LOCKED STYLE]</span>, <span class="prompt-neg">--ar [RATIO] --no [EXCLUSIONS]</span></div> </div> <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);margin-top:10px;"><strong>Rule:</strong> The client pays for everything in [LOCKED]. Individual images only change [SUBJECT] and [ACTION]. Everything else is fixed for the entire campaign.</p> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 31: Brand Photography Style Guide',emoji:'🎨',desc:'Build a complete visual style guide for a brand. Lock every photographic variable. Generate 3 test images to prove consistency. This is a real deliverable clients pay for.',deliverables:['Brand overview: who they are, who they serve, one-word brand personality','Locked visual variables: palette, lighting, surface, lens, aspect ratio','3 test prompts using the style guide (swap subject only)','Does the system hold? What would you tighten?'],placeholder:'Brand overview:\nPersonality word:\n\nLocked variables:\nPalette:\nLighting:\nSurface:\nLens:\nAR:\n\nTest prompt 1:\nTest prompt 2:\nTest prompt 3:\n\nConsistency holds (yes/partially/no):\nWhat I\'d tighten:',systemPrompt:'You are a brand photography director reviewing a student\'s style guide system. Evaluate: are the locked variables specific enough to reproduce? Would a different person generate the same visual style from these rules? Point out the vaguest variable and how to make it airtight. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[32]={
  phase:'Phase 4 — Brand Systems',title:'Day 32: Color Theory for AI Brands',
  tools:['Midjourney v8','Figma'],time:'60 min',tags:['color','theory','palette','psychology'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Color Theory for Designers — Complete Foundation',channel:'The Futur',duration:'41:20',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=color+theory+designers+complete+foundation+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#f7971e,#ffd200)'},
    {title:'Brand Color Psychology — Why Colors Make You Buy',channel:'Sagi Haviv',duration:'24:10',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brand+color+psychology+why+colors+make+you+buy',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#667eea,#764ba2)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Coolors.co',desc:'The best free color palette tool. Generate, adjust, and export brand palettes with HEX/RGB/CMYK values.',type:'website',url:'https://coolors.co',tag:'Tool'},
    {name:'Brand Colors — Famous Brand Palettes',desc:'See the exact color palettes of 1000+ famous brands. Understand what colors the world\'s best brands use.',type:'website',url:'https://brandcolors.net',tag:'Reference'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#f59e0b22;color:#f59e0b">🎨</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Color Is the Fastest Brand Signal in the World</div><div class="concept-card-sub">Recognition before conscious thought — that\'s what color does</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Humans recognize color in 90 milliseconds — faster than they read a word or process a shape. This means your brand\'s color communicates before any other element. The goal of brand color is not to look nice. It is to trigger a specific emotional and associative response so reliably that over time the color alone identifies the brand. Coca-Cola Red. Tiffany Blue. Hermès Orange.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#fef9e7;border-color:#f59e0b"><span class="hb-icon">🧠</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">Color Psychology — The Core Associations</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>Blue:</strong> Trust, technology, precision, calm. Used by: Facebook, Samsung, IBM, Oral-B. Almost every bank in the world.<br><strong>Red:</strong> Energy, urgency, passion, appetite. Used by: Coca-Cola, Netflix, YouTube, Ferrari.<br><strong>Green:</strong> Health, nature, growth, permission. Used by: Whole Foods, Starbucks, Spotify.<br><strong>Black:</strong> Luxury, authority, sophistication. Used by: Chanel, Rolex, Apple (partially).<br><strong>Orange:</strong> Warmth, energy, accessibility, friendliness. Used by: Amazon, Hermès, Harley-Davidson.<br><strong>Purple:</strong> Creativity, royalty, spirituality. Used by: Cadbury, Hallmark, FedEx.<br><strong>Yellow/Gold:</strong> Optimism, warmth, premium. Used by: McDonald\'s, IKEA, Rolex (gold).<br><strong>White:</strong> Simplicity, cleanliness, minimalism. Used by: Apple, Tesla, many luxury brands as primary background.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#2563eb22;color:#2563eb">📐</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Building a Brand Palette — The 60/30/10 Rule</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The formula that makes every brand look professionally designed</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Professional brand palettes follow a proportion rule: <strong>60% dominant color</strong> (your brand\'s primary background or most used color), <strong>30% secondary color</strong> (the main supporting color that creates harmony), and <strong>10% accent color</strong> (the pop that draws attention to key elements). This proportion is used by virtually every professional branding agency in the world.</p><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#2563eb">60</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">60% — Dominant (Backgrounds, Large Areas)</div><div class="step-desc">Usually the neutral or most associated color. For a wellness brand: warm cream. For a tech brand: white or dark navy. This fills most of every design and creates the immediate brand feeling.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#2563eb">30</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">30% — Secondary (Supporting, Typography Areas)</div><div class="step-desc">Harmonizes with the dominant. Often the brand\'s most recognizable "signature" color. Sage green as the 30% makes cream feel like a wellness brand. Deep navy as 30% makes white feel like a finance brand.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#2563eb">10</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">10% — Accent (CTAs, Key Details, Moments of Emphasis)</div><div class="step-desc">Used sparingly — maximum impact. Often contrasting or complementary. If dominant is cream, the accent might be warm terracotta or dusty gold. This is what makes buttons, highlights, and key moments stand out.</div></div></li></ul></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon blue">🎨</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Locking Your Palette Into AI Tools at Scale</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The technique that makes 50 brand images look like one photoshoot</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Knowing your brand palette is step one. Enforcing it across 50 AI-generated images is step two — and it requires specific techniques per tool.</p><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#2563eb">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Midjourney — Name Colours + Seed Lock</div><div class="step-desc">Name your palette colours precisely in every prompt: "warm cream (#F5F0E8), sage green (#8B9E7A), terracotta accent (#C4704F)." Hex codes are partially understood by v7 and improve colour accuracy. Combine with --seed [number] to lock colour temperature across the entire campaign batch.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#2563eb">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Midjourney --sref for Palette Consistency</div><div class="step-desc">Generate ONE perfect on-palette image first. React with the envelope emoji to get its URL. Then use --sref [URL] in all subsequent prompts. Midjourney extracts the colour palette, texture, and light quality from that reference image and applies it to every new generation. Adjust influence with --sw 0-1000.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#2563eb">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Adobe Firefly — Colour Wheel Constraints</div><div class="step-desc">Firefly has a native Colour Wheel tool in the generation interface. Input your exact hex values and Firefly applies them as generative constraints. Combined with Style Reference images, this gives maximum palette consistency — and produces commercially licensed output for agency client work.</div></div></li></ul><div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// PALETTE-LOCKED MIDJOURNEY CAMPAIGN PROMPT</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this,\'[PRODUCT] in [CONTEXT], warm cream and sage green colour palette, terracotta accent detail, single directional window light from left, 85mm lens shallow depth of field, Kinfolk minimal aesthetic --ar 4:5 --v 7 --sref [YOUR_REFERENCE_IMAGE_URL] --sw 800 --seed 3847291 --no cool tones, blue cast, multiple light sources, text, watermark\')">📋 Copy</button></div><div class="prompt-card-body">[PRODUCT] in [CONTEXT], warm cream and sage green palette, terracotta accent, window light from left <strong>--sref [URL] --sw 800 --seed [NUMBER]</strong> --no cool tones, blue cast, multiple light sources</div></div></div></div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 32: Build 3 Brand Color Palettes',emoji:'🎨',desc:'Design 3 brand palettes targeting 3 different emotional responses using the 60/30/10 rule. Then generate one Midjourney image per palette to prove it works visually.',deliverables:['Palette 1: Trust + professionalism → hex codes + image prompt','Palette 2: Energy + excitement → hex codes + image prompt','Palette 3: Calm + luxury → hex codes + image prompt','Which palette was hardest to get right — and why?'],placeholder:'Palette 1 (Trust):\nHex codes (60/30/10):\nMidjourney prompt:\n\nPalette 2 (Energy):\nHex codes:\nMidjourney prompt:\n\nPalette 3 (Calm/Luxury):\nHex codes:\nMidjourney prompt:\n\nHardest palette and why:',systemPrompt:'You are a brand color strategist and visual identity designer. Review the student\'s 3 palettes. Do the hex codes actually create the stated emotional response? Are they applying 60/30/10 correctly? Give specific feedback on one palette that feels off and how to fix it. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[33]={
  phase:'Phase 4 — Brand Systems',title:'Day 33: Typography Systems',
  tools:['Ideogram 2.0','Figma'],time:'60 min',tags:['typography','type','hierarchy','brand'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Typography for Non-Designers — Complete Foundation',channel:'The Futur',duration:'44:20',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=typography+non+designers+complete+foundation+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1a1a,#4a4a4a)'},
    {title:'Brand Typography System — Choosing and Pairing Fonts',channel:'Sagi Haviv',duration:'26:30',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brand+typography+system+pairing+fonts+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#2c3e50,#4ca1af)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Google Fonts — Type Specimen',desc:'Free, high-quality typefaces with pairing suggestions. Every professional needs to know these.',type:'website',url:'https://fonts.google.com',tag:'Tool'},
    {name:'Typewolf — Font in Use',desc:'See which fonts the world\'s best brands use in real applications. Curated and categorized by style.',type:'website',url:'https://www.typewolf.com',tag:'Reference'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#1a1a1a22;color:#1a1a1a">Aa</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Typography is 90% of Design</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The invisible infrastructure of every brand</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Massimo Vignelli, one of the greatest designers of the 20th century, said he could design everything with just 6 typefaces. Typography is not about having many fonts — it is about understanding the <strong>personality and function</strong> of each typeface you choose, and using contrast, hierarchy, and scale to communicate meaning before the reader reads a single word.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#f8fafc;border-color:#1a1a1a"><span class="hb-icon">📚</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The 4 Typeface Categories — Know These Cold</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>Serif (Times New Roman, Playfair, Garamond):</strong> History, authority, luxury, trust. The strokes have "feet." Traditional media, law firms, heritage brands, editorial publishing.<br><strong>Sans-serif (Helvetica, DM Sans, Inter):</strong> Modern, clean, democratic, digital. No feet. Technology, health, startups, UI. Every tech brand uses a sans-serif.<br><strong>Slab Serif (Rockwell, Clarendon):</strong> Strong, bold, confident. Advertising, sports, American Americana. Makes headlines feel powerful.<br><strong>Script/Display (handwritten, decorative):</strong> Personal, artisan, premium. Beauty brands, cafes, wedding industry. Never use for body copy — only headlines and accents.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#2563eb22;color:#2563eb">📐</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">The Type Pairing Rule — And Why It Works</div><div class="concept-card-sub">Max 2 typefaces. Make them contrast. Give each a job.</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>The golden rule of typography: <strong>maximum 2 typefaces per brand system</strong>. One for headlines (personality, emotion, brand voice), one for body text (readability, neutrality, function). The pairing works through contrast — if both are similar, there\'s no hierarchy. If they contrast, each has a role and the system has harmony.</p><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#2563eb">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Pairing Rule: Contrast Category</div><div class="step-desc">Pair a Serif with a Sans-serif. Never pair two serifs or two sans-serifs of similar weight. Classic pairing: Playfair Display (headline) + DM Sans (body). Sophisticated, editorial, clear hierarchy.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#2563eb">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Hierarchy Through Scale, Not Different Fonts</div><div class="step-desc">H1 (headline): 48–80px, bold weight. H2: 28–36px, medium. H3: 20–24px, medium. Body: 16–18px, regular. Caption: 12–14px, light. Use size and weight before reaching for a new font.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#2563eb">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Leading (Line Spacing) Rule</div><div class="step-desc">Body text: line height = font size × 1.5. Headlines: line height = font size × 1.1–1.2. Tight leading makes headlines feel powerful. Loose leading makes body text feel luxurious. These are the invisible signals of professional typography.</div></div></li></ul></div></div> <div class="compare-section"> <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:800;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:10px;">🔤 Same Content — 2 Typography Systems</div> <div class="compare-toggle-bar"> <button class="compare-tab bad active" id="cmp33-a" onclick="switchCompare(\'cmp33\',\'bad\')">Premium/Editorial</button> <button class="compare-tab good" id="cmp33-b" onclick="switchCompare(\'cmp33\',\'good\')">Modern/Digital</button> </div> <div class="compare-panel bad-panel active" id="cmp33-a-panel"> <div class="compare-prompt">Brand: Luxury skincare. Headline: "The Morning Ritual." System: Garamond Display (high-contrast serif) + Freight Text body. Ideogram prompt: "luxury skincare poster, GARAMOND style serif headline, thin elegant letterforms, wide tracking, centered layout, generous white space, cream background"</div> <div class="compare-annotation"><strong>Why it works:</strong> High-contrast serifs signal heritage, quality, craft. The thick-thin stroke contrast communicates luxury without saying it. Garamond history spans 500 years — that history transfers to the brand.</div> </div> <div class="compare-panel good-panel" id="cmp33-b-panel"> <div class="compare-prompt">Brand: Fintech app. Headline: "Money, Simplified." System: Inter Bold (geometric sans) + Inter Regular body. Ideogram prompt: "fintech poster, BOLD GEOMETRIC SANS-SERIF headline, clean modern grid, left-aligned editorial, electric blue accent, white background, Swiss design system"</div> <div class="compare-annotation"><strong>Why it works:</strong> Geometric sans-serifs (Inter, Neue Haas, Aktiv Grotesk) signal efficiency, technology, transparency. Zero ornamentation. The grid communicates systematic thinking — exactly what users want from their money app.</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon amber">📐</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">The 3 Font Pairing Rules</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">Why some pairs work and others create visual chaos</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Contrast in style, harmony in weight</div><div class="step-desc">Pair a serif headline with a sans-serif body (contrast in style). Keep similar weight — do not pair ultra-thin headline with bold body. Headline can be bolder, but body must never outshout it.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Max 2 typefaces in any design</div><div class="step-desc">One headline family. One body family. For more variety, use weights within the same family (Bold, Regular, Light). Three different typefaces almost always creates visual noise.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Test at small size first</div><div class="step-desc">A typeface beautiful at 72px may be unreadable at 12px. Always check body font at 14px and 12px before committing. Most display fonts fail this test — they are display only, not text fonts.</div></div></li> </ul> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 33: Typography System — 2 Brands',emoji:'Aa',desc:'Design complete typography systems for 2 opposite brand personalities. Show your work: font pairing logic, hierarchy, and how to translate each into Ideogram prompts.',deliverables:['Brand 1 (premium/editorial): headline font, body font, pairing logic','Brand 2 (modern/digital): headline font, body font, pairing logic','Ideogram prompt for Brand 1 using your typography system','Ideogram prompt for Brand 2 using your typography system'],placeholder:'Brand 1 (premium):\nHeadline font (personality):\nBody font:\nWhy they pair:\nIdeogram prompt:\n\nBrand 2 (modern/digital):\nHeadline font:\nBody font:\nWhy they pair:\nIdeogram prompt:',systemPrompt:'You are a typography director specializing in brand identity. Review the student\'s 2 type systems. Do the pairings follow typographic logic (contrast in style, harmony in weight)? Are they translating font personalities into Ideogram-readable descriptions correctly? Give specific pairing feedback. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[34]={
  phase:'Phase 4 — Brand Systems',title:'Day 34: Logo & Identity with AI',
  tools:['Midjourney v8','Ideogram 2.0','Adobe Firefly'],time:'65 min',tags:['logo','identity','design','AI'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Logo Design with AI — Professional Process 2025',channel:'The Futur',duration:'36:20',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=logo+design+AI+professional+process+midjourney+ideogram+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1a1a,#6c6c6c)'},
    {title:'Logo Concepts in Midjourney — What Works and What Doesn\'t',channel:'Theoretically Media',duration:'22:40',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=logo+design+midjourney+concepts+professional+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#667eea,#764ba2)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Logopond — Logo Inspiration',desc:'Community of professional logo designers. Study the simplicity, scalability, and concept behind the best logos.',type:'website',url:'https://logopond.com',tag:'Reference'},
    {name:'Aaron Draplin — Logo Design Love',desc:'The most accessible and inspiring book on logo design thinking. Study his process before generating.',type:'website',url:'https://www.draplin.com',tag:'Thinking'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#1a1a1a22;color:#1a1a1a">⬟</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">What Makes a Logo Work — The 5 Criteria</div><div class="concept-card-sub">AI can generate logos. Only you can judge if they work.</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>AI models produce interesting shapes and combinations. What they cannot do is evaluate whether a logo actually functions as a brand mark. You need to learn these 5 criteria so you can judge AI output critically and select, refine, or discard with professional confidence.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#f8fafc;border-color:#1a1a1a"><span class="hb-icon">✅</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The 5 Logo Success Criteria</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>1. Works at all sizes:</strong> Legible at 16px favicon AND at billboard scale. Intricate detail = logo failure at small sizes.<br><strong>2. Works in 1 color:</strong> Remove all color. Does the shape still communicate the brand? If it dies without color, the concept is weak.<br><strong>3. Has a concept:</strong> Not just a "nice shape" — there should be a specific, explainable idea behind the mark. The FedEx arrow. The Amazon smile/arrow. Nike\'s forward motion.<br><strong>4. Distinguishable in category:</strong> Test against competitors. Does it stand out or blend in? A tech startup that looks like every other tech startup fails this test.<br><strong>5. Timeless, not trendy:</strong> The Chanel interlocked C was designed in 1925 and looks current today. Avoid gradients, 3D effects, or any treatment that will feel dated in 3 years.</div></div></div><div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// LOGO CONCEPTS — MIDJOURNEY APPROACH</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this,\'Minimal logo design concept for a premium audio brand called Resonance. Explore: abstract sound wave simplified to 3–5 lines, single color black on white, geometric precision, scalable icon with letter R integration, clean vector-ready aesthetic, flat design no gradients no shadows, white background --no realistic photo texture gradient 3D effect --ar 1:1 --v 7\')">📋 Copy</button></div><div class="prompt-card-body">Minimal logo design concept for a premium audio brand called Resonance. Explore: abstract sound wave simplified to 3–5 lines, single color black on white, geometric precision, scalable icon with letter R integration, clean vector-ready aesthetic, flat design no gradients no shadows, white background --no realistic photo texture gradient 3D effect --ar 1:1 --v 7</div></div></div></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon green">📐</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">The AI Logo Design Workflow</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">AI generates concepts. You make the decisions. Here is the exact process.</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Generate 3 directions, not 1</div><div class="step-desc">Never show a client your first AI logo output. Generate at least 3 different conceptual directions (wordmark vs icon vs combination mark). This gives you a real choice and demonstrates strategic thinking.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Evaluate in black and white first</div><div class="step-desc">Color creates emotional responses that override design critique. If the shape does not work in black and white, color will not save it. Always evaluate logos in monochrome before considering color.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Test at favicon size</div><div class="step-desc">Reduce your logo to 16x16px. Does it still work? Modern logos must work at this scale — app icons, browser tabs, and social profile pictures all require it. Complex logos fail completely.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">AI for exploration, Figma for refinement</div><div class="step-desc">AI generates the direction. Figma or Illustrator refines it to vector. Never deliver an AI raster image as a final logo — logos must be vector for scalability. The AI output is your concept sketch.</div></div></li> </ul> <div class="prompt-card"> <div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// LOGO EXPLORATION — WORDMARK</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this, \'Minimal wordmark logo concept for premium wellness brand named SOVA, clean geometric letterforms with subtle organic curve detail on the S, monochrome black on white, Swiss design principles, negative space used intentionally, lettermark feels both modern and timeless, vector-style clean edges, isolated on white background --ar 1:1 --no color gradients texture effects shadow\')">📋 Copy</button></div> <div class="prompt-card-body">Minimal wordmark for SOVA, <span class="prompt-param">geometric letterforms, organic curve on S</span>, monochrome, <span class="prompt-style">Swiss design, negative space, vector-clean edges</span> --ar 1:1</div> </div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon green">🔧</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">Tool Spotlight: Ideogram 2.0 for Logo Concepts</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">The only AI tool where your brand name renders accurately</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <p><strong>Ideogram 2.0</strong> — Renders text accurately in logos. "Minimal wordmark for brand SOVA" → Ideogram writes SOVA correctly. Use Ideogram for wordmark exploration, Figma for vector refinement. <a href="https://ideogram.ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--primary);font-weight:700;">Open Ideogram →</a></p> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 34: Logo Concept from a Brand Brief',emoji:'⬟',desc:'Use a real brand brief to generate and evaluate AI logo concepts. Test multiple directions. Apply the 5 criteria to choose the strongest.',deliverables:['Use this brief: \'Meridian — premium fitness app for women 30+. Intelligent, not intense. Minimal, not cold.\'','Generate 3 different logo directions — describe each concept','Score each against the 5 criteria: Simple / Scalable / Memorable / Versatile / Appropriate','Which direction wins — and what would you refine in a next iteration?'],placeholder:'Direction 1:\nConcept:\n5-criteria score:\n\nDirection 2:\nConcept:\n5-criteria score:\n\nDirection 3:\nConcept:\n5-criteria score:\n\nWinner:\nRefinement for next iteration:',systemPrompt:'You are a brand identity designer reviewing AI-assisted logo concepts. Evaluate the student\'s 3 directions against the brief. Do they reflect intelligence and minimalism without coldness? Is the winning choice genuinely the strongest? Give specific visual refinement suggestions. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[35]={
  phase:'Phase 4 — Brand Systems',title:'Day 35: Social Media Content System',
  tools:['Midjourney v8','ChatGPT Images','Figma'],time:'65 min',tags:['social','content','system','canva'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Social Media Content System — AI Brand Strategy 2025',channel:'The Futur',duration:'32:10',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=social+media+content+system+AI+brand+strategy+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#f7971e,#ffd200)'},
    {title:'Figma — Design System at Scale',channel:'Matt Wolfe',duration:'21:30',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=canva+AI+content+system+scale+brand+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#00c9ff,#92fe9d)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Pinterest: Brand Instagram Feed Aesthetics',desc:'Study the most cohesive brand Instagram feeds — every post is planned as part of a system, not individually.',type:'pinterest',url:'https://pinterest.com/search/pins/?q=brand+instagram+feed+aesthetic+consistent+2026',tag:'Reference'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#f59e0b22;color:#f59e0b">📱</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">A Content System vs Random Posting</div><div class="concept-card-sub">System = 10x output, 10x consistency, 10x less effort</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Most social media accounts post reactively — they make individual content decisions for every post. A professional content system makes all the decisions once, at the system level, and then produces individual posts rapidly within those rules. The result: a feed that looks like a magazine, not a mood board of random ideas.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#fef9e7;border-color:#f59e0b"><span class="hb-icon">📐</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The 5 Content Pillars System</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>What it is:</strong> Every brand has 5 defined themes (pillars) it posts about. Every post belongs to one of these 5 pillars. The pillars ensure variety without randomness.<br><br><strong>Example — skincare brand pillars:</strong><br>1. Education (ingredient deep-dives, how-to routines)<br>2. Product (hero shots, reveals, new launches)<br>3. Lifestyle (who the customer is, their world)<br>4. Community (UGC reposts, testimonials, DM screenshots)<br>5. Values (sustainability, sourcing, behind the scenes)<br><br>Post in rotation: 1-2-3-4-5-1-2-3... The feed never repeats and always feels coherent.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#2563eb22;color:#2563eb">⚙️</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Building the AI Content Production Pipeline</div><div class="concept-card-sub">From brief to 30 posts per month in under 4 hours</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>With an AI content pipeline, a single designer can produce a month of brand content in under 4 hours. The key is building the pipeline once and operating it repeatedly. Here is the professional workflow:</p><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#f59e0b">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Monthly Brief (30 mins)</div><div class="step-desc">Define this month\'s hero product, key messages, seasonal moments, and content goals. Write a one-page brief that all AI prompts will reference.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#f59e0b">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Image Generation Batch (60 mins)</div><div class="step-desc">Generate 40–50 images in Midjourney using your locked brand template. More than you need — you\'ll cull later. Batch all generations at once to stay in the same creative headspace.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#f59e0b">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Figma Template Production (60 mins)</div><div class="step-desc">Import best images into your Figma brand templates. Each pillar has a template. Swap images and copy. 30 posts in 60 minutes if templates are built correctly.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#f59e0b">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Copy Writing with Claude (30 mins)</div><div class="step-desc">Write captions and CTAs for all 30 posts using Claude. Prompt: "Write 30 Instagram captions for [brand] in [brand voice]. Each caption should match this pillar: [list]." Export as CSV and bulk-upload to scheduling tool.</div></div></li></ul></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon blue">⚡</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">The 1-Day Batch System</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">One day of work = 30 days of content. The exact workflow agencies use.</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">AM</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Morning: Generate All Images (2-3 hours)</div><div class="step-desc">Run all Midjourney/ChatGPT Images prompts in parallel. Use your brand system template — swap only subject/action. Generate 40 images to have 30 usable ones. Queue all prompts at once and let them run while you write copy.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">PM</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Afternoon: Copy + Captions (2 hours)</div><div class="step-desc">Write all 30 captions in one session using ChatGPT: "Write 10 captions for a [industry] brand in [voice] tone for [pillar name] content." Edit and personalize. Batch in one document.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">EVE</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Evening: Assemble in Figma (1.5 hours)</div><div class="step-desc">Use your 3 locked Figma templates. Import all images. Apply the brand template. Export all 30. Upload to a scheduler (Later, Buffer, or Meta Business Suite). Schedule across 30 days.</div></div></li> </ul> <div class="highlight-box green"> <span class="hb-icon">💰</span> <div class="hb-content"> <div class="hb-title">This Is a Productized Service</div> <div class="hb-text">You just described a service agencies sell for 15,000-40,000 rupees per month as "Social Media Management." Your AI workflow compresses the production time to 6-7 hours. That gap is your margin.</div> </div> </div> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 35: 30-Post Content System Blueprint',emoji:'📱',desc:'Design the infrastructure for a 30-post content month — pillars, templates, AI image integration. This is what content agencies charge thousands for.',deliverables:['Brand + 3 content pillars (Educate / Inspire / Sell or equivalent)','One Figma/AI template per pillar (describe layout and visual style)','3 sample post prompts — one per pillar — with AI image prompt','How would you batch-create this in one day per week?'],placeholder:'Brand:\nPillar 1:\nPillar 2:\nPillar 3:\n\nTemplate 1 layout:\nTemplate 2 layout:\nTemplate 3 layout:\n\nPost prompt + image prompt (Pillar 1):\nPost prompt + image prompt (Pillar 2):\nPost prompt + image prompt (Pillar 3):\n\nBatch workflow plan:',systemPrompt:'You are a social media content strategist and AI design director. Review the student\'s content system. Are the pillars strategically differentiated? Do the templates actually enable consistent batch production? Give specific feedback on making the system more scalable. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[36]={
  phase:'Phase 4 — Brand Systems',title:'Day 36: Packaging Design with AI',
  tools:['Midjourney v8','Adobe Firefly'],time:'65 min',tags:['packaging','product','design','3D'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Packaging Design AI — Concept to 3D Mockup',channel:'Theoretically Media',duration:'24:30',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=packaging+design+AI+concept+3D+mockup+midjourney+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#667eea,#764ba2)'},
    {title:'Dieline Packaging Design Principles for AI Designers',channel:'Dieline',duration:'28:10',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=packaging+design+principles+dieline+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#f7971e,#ffd200)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Dieline — Packaging Design Awards',desc:'The world\'s leading packaging design publication. Study award-winning packaging and understand why each works.',type:'website',url:'https://thedieline.com',tag:'Benchmark'},
    {name:'Pinterest: Premium Packaging Design',desc:'High-end product packaging — skincare, food, tech accessories. Study materials, colors, and structural design.',type:'pinterest',url:'https://pinterest.com/search/pins/?q=premium+product+packaging+design+2026',tag:'Reference'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#8b5cf622;color:#8b5cf6">📦</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Packaging is the Last Point of Sale</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The physical brand experience — when the customer finally touches the brand</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Of all brand touchpoints, packaging is unique: it is the <strong>one moment when the customer physically holds the brand</strong>. Digital ads are seen, not touched. Packaging is experienced with multiple senses — sight, touch, sound (unboxing), sometimes smell. This is why packaging has disproportionate impact on brand loyalty and premium perception.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#f3e8ff;border-color:#a855f7"><span class="hb-icon">🎯</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The 4 Packaging Design Questions</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>1. Shelf visibility:</strong> Does it stand out in a lineup of 20 competitors? Color, structure, and scale determine this.<br><strong>2. Tactile signal:</strong> What does the material say? Matte = premium, minimal. Gloss = commercial, accessible. Kraft paper = eco, artisan. Soft-touch laminate = ultra-luxury.<br><strong>3. Information hierarchy:</strong> Brand name (largest), product name, key benefit, details. In that order. The customer should know what it is in 0.5 seconds.<br><strong>4. Unboxing experience:</strong> The inside matters as much as the outside for premium brands. Tissue paper, ribbon, a handwritten note, a scent — these are the moments that create social sharing.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#10b98122;color:#10b981">🖥</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Prompting Packaging Visualization in Midjourney</div><div class="concept-card-sub">Flat design → 3D mockup — the professional presentation workflow</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Midjourney cannot generate accurate text on packaging reliably. But it excels at generating <strong>packaging concepts with correct structure, material, and environmental context</strong> — which is exactly what you need for concept presentation to a client. The workflow: prompt the 3D visualization, then add actual text in Figma or Photoshop.</p><div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// PACKAGING VISUALIZATION — MIDJOURNEY</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this,\'Product packaging visualization, premium glass skincare serum bottle 100ml with matte black metal cap, minimalist label design in warm cream with fine serif typography placeholder, placed on dark wet marble surface, single overhead rim light with soft diffused fill, condensation droplets on glass, La Mer Aesop packaging aesthetic, product photography quality, --ar 4:5 --v 7\')">📋 Copy</button></div><div class="prompt-card-body">Product packaging visualization, premium glass skincare serum bottle 100ml with matte black metal cap, minimalist label design in warm cream with fine serif typography placeholder, placed on dark wet marble surface, single overhead rim light with soft diffused fill, condensation droplets on glass, La Mer Aesop packaging aesthetic, product photography quality, --ar 4:5 --v 7</div></div></div></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon amber">📦</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">The 4 Packaging Problem Questions</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">Answer these before generating a single image</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">What is the format?</div><div class="step-desc">Box, pouch, tube, bottle, can, sleeve, jar? Each has structural constraints. A luxury serum goes in a glass dropper bottle. A protein powder goes in a matte stand-up pouch. The format communicates price point before the consumer reads anything.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">What is the primary material?</div><div class="step-desc">Glass vs plastic vs kraft paper vs metal. Glass = premium, heavy. Matte black plastic = premium-lite. Kraft paper = sustainable, artisan. Aluminum = sustainable + premium. Your AI visualization must specify material or it defaults to generic plastic.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">What is the hero visual?</div><div class="step-desc">Illustration? Photography? Pure typography? Texture pattern? Premium brands often use negative space (minimal, confident). Mass-market brands use photography. Craft brands use illustration. Pick one approach — not all three.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">What must it communicate in 2 seconds?</div><div class="step-desc">What/flavor/variant first. Brand name second. Benefit third. Everything else is detail. On shelf, you have 2 seconds and 30cm of viewing distance. Your packaging must win in that moment before any text is read.</div></div></li> </ul> <div class="prompt-card"> <div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// PACKAGING VISUALIZATION PROMPT</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this, \'3D product packaging visualization, luxury face serum, matte black glass dropper bottle 50ml, gold metallic embossed brand name on front, minimal cream label with elegant thin-weight serif typography, bottle on white marble surface, soft studio lighting, beauty editorial photography style, ultra-sharp foreground focus, photorealistic render --ar 4:5 --no busy design multiple colors text clutter\')">📋 Copy</button></div> <div class="prompt-card-body">Luxury serum, <span class="prompt-param">matte black glass dropper 50ml</span>, gold embossed brand name, <span class="prompt-style">minimal cream label, thin serif, marble surface, beauty editorial</span> --ar 4:5</div> </div> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 36: Packaging Design Concept',emoji:'📦',desc:'Design a packaging concept from brief to 3D visualization. Solve the structural, visual, and brand problems in sequence.',deliverables:['Product category + target market + price positioning','Answer the 4 packaging questions: Format? Material? Hero Visual? Typography Style?','Your AI image prompt for the 3D product visualization','What does the packaging communicate before the consumer reads a word?'],placeholder:'Product:\nTarget market:\nPrice positioning:\n\nFormat:\nMaterial:\nHero visual:\nTypography style:\n\nAI visualization prompt:\n\nFirst impression (no words read):',systemPrompt:'You are a packaging design director. Review the student\'s packaging concept. Does the format match the product category? Does the visual hierarchy communicate the brand positioning before any text is read? Give feedback on what a packaging manufacturer would flag as impractical. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[37]={
  phase:'Phase 4 — Brand Systems',title:'Day 37: Motion Design Principles',
  tools:['Kling 2.6','CapCut Pro','Google Flow'],time:'65 min',tags:['motion','animation','transitions','brand'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Motion Design Principles — 12 Rules of Animation for Brands',channel:'The Futur',duration:'38:50',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=motion+design+principles+12+rules+animation+brand+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#667eea,#764ba2)'},
    {title:'Brand Motion Design — AI + CapCut Workflow',channel:'Theoretically Media',duration:'23:10',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brand+motion+design+AI+capcut+workflow+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#f7971e,#ffd200)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Motionographer — Brand Motion',desc:'Award-winning brand motion design. Study the easing, timing, and purposeful use of motion.',type:'website',url:'https://motionographer.com',tag:'Benchmark'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#8b5cf622;color:#8b5cf6">🌊</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Motion Has a Grammar — Learn It Before You Animate</div><div class="concept-card-sub">Easing, timing, and purpose — the invisible language of great motion</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Bad motion design is immediately recognizable: elements appear without easing, transitions feel mechanical, and animations happen for no purpose. Good motion design is invisible — you feel the brand personality without consciously noticing the animation. The difference is understanding <strong>easing, timing, and motivated motion</strong>.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#f3e8ff;border-color:#a855f7"><span class="hb-icon">🎯</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The 3 Core Motion Principles</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>1. Easing:</strong> Nothing in nature moves at constant speed. It accelerates out and decelerates in (ease in-out). Luxury brands use slow ease-out (the motion lingers). Energetic brands use fast ease-in (the motion snaps in with momentum).<br><strong>2. Timing:</strong> Luxury = 600–1200ms transitions. Tech/youth = 200–400ms. This is not a preference — it is a brand personality signal. Apply consistently.<br><strong>3. Motivated motion:</strong> Every animation should answer "why is this moving?" If the answer is "because it looks cool," remove it. If the answer is "to reveal information," "to guide the eye," or "to signal a state change" — keep it.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#f59e0b22;color:#f59e0b">⚙️</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Applying Motion Principles in AI Video Prompts</div><div class="concept-card-sub">Translate motion grammar into AI prompt language</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>When you write Kling or Runway prompts, you are defining motion behavior. Understanding easing and timing helps you choose the right language to get the motion quality you need.</p><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#8b5cf6">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Luxury Easing in Prompts</div><div class="step-desc">Prompt language: "motion decelerates gently as it approaches final position," "camera glides with imperceptible deceleration," "fabric settles unhurriedly." These signal ease-out behavior to the AI.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#8b5cf6">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Energetic Easing in Prompts</div><div class="step-desc">Prompt language: "rapid snap to position," "explosive initial motion with abrupt stop," "fast entry from off-frame with momentum." These signal ease-in behavior.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#8b5cf6">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Motivated Motion</div><div class="step-desc">Before adding any animation in CapCut or any motion in an AI video prompt, ask: what is this motion FOR? Write the answer as the first clause of your prompt. "Camera pushes in slowly TO reveal the detail of the texture" — the purpose is in the prompt.</div></div></li></ul></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon blue">🎬</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">Easing Vocabulary for AI Video Prompts</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">The specific words that control how motion feels — not looks</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">🏆</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Luxury Easing Words</div><div class="step-desc">Slow deceleration into rest. Gradual ease-out. Floats to a stop. Decelerates from momentum. Silk-smooth deceleration. The object never snaps or jumps — it glides to its final position over 2-3 times longer than expected.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">⚡</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Energy / Tech Easing Words</div><div class="step-desc">Snappy ease-in-out. Fast acceleration into slow precision stop. Mechanical click to position. Elastic overshoot and settle. Burst of speed then decisive stop. Confident, fast, precise — the opposite of luxury.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">🌿</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Organic / Wellness Easing Words</div><div class="step-desc">Breathes into position. Undulates naturally. No hard stops — every motion continues slightly past center then gently settles. Like a branch swaying in wind. Never mechanical, never rushed.</div></div></li> </ul> <div class="prompt-card"> <div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// LUXURY EASING — KLING PROMPT</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this, \'Luxury perfume bottle floating upward from white surface, slow graceful acceleration from rest, silk-smooth continuous rise with gradual deceleration at peak height, bottle hangs weightlessly for 2 seconds, then descends with identical slow deceleration back to surface, no bouncing, no mechanical movement, continuous silky motion throughout\')">📋 Copy</button></div> <div class="prompt-card-body">Bottle <span class="prompt-param">slow graceful acceleration from rest</span>, <span class="prompt-style">silk-smooth rise with gradual deceleration</span>, <span class="prompt-param">hangs weightlessly, no bouncing, continuous silky motion</span></div> </div> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 37: Motion Easing — 2 Brand Feels',emoji:'🌊',desc:'Create the same product reveal in two opposite easing styles: luxury (slow deceleration) vs energy (fast acceleration). Show how timing IS brand personality.',deliverables:['Product chosen','Luxury easing prompt: slow, decelerating, restrained — specific speed words used','Energy easing prompt: fast, accelerating, punchy — specific speed words used','What 3 specific words changed between the two versions?'],placeholder:'Product:\n\nLuxury easing prompt:\nSpeed words used:\n\nEnergy easing prompt:\nSpeed words used:\n\n3 words that changed:\n1.\n2.\n3.\n\nWhy easing = brand personality:',systemPrompt:'You are a motion design director specializing in brand animation. Review the student\'s easing contrast. Did they use specific timing language (gradual deceleration vs rapid burst) or just mood words? Give 3 precise motion vocabulary terms they should add to each version. Max 220 words.'}
};

LESSONS[38]={
  phase:'Phase 4 — Brand Systems',title:'Day 38: Complete Brand Package Delivery',
  tools:['Midjourney v8','Figma'],time:'90 min',tags:['brand','package','delivery','client'],
  videos:[
    {title:'How to Present Brand Identity to Clients — Professional',channel:'The Futur',duration:'44:30',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how+to+present+brand+identity+clients+professional+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#2c3e50,#4ca1af)'},
    {title:'Brand Guidelines Document — What to Include',channel:'Sagi Haviv',duration:'29:20',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brand+guidelines+document+what+to+include+professional+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1a1a,#6c6c6c)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Brand Style Guide Examples — Best in Class',desc:'Real brand guideline documents from companies like Mailchimp, Airbnb, and NASA. See the professional standard.',type:'website',url:'https://www.canva.com/learn/brand-style-guide-examples/',tag:'Benchmark'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#10b98122;color:#10b981">📋</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Phase 4 Capstone — What a Complete Brand Package Contains</div><div class="concept-card-sub">Everything a client needs to run their brand without you</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>The brand package is the deliverable that turns a project into a system. A client who has a complete brand package can brief any designer, photographer, or social media manager — and get consistent results without you being in the room. That is the test of a complete brand package: <strong>can someone else operate this brand correctly without calling you?</strong></p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#d1fae5;border-color:#10b981"><span class="hb-icon">📐</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The 8-Section Brand Package</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>1. Brand Strategy (1 page):</strong> Positioning, target audience, brand personality (3 adjectives), what the brand stands for.<br><strong>2. Logo System:</strong> Primary, horizontal, icon-only. With clear space rules and minimum sizes.<br><strong>3. Color Palette:</strong> All colors with HEX, RGB, CMYK. Usage rules (when to use which).<br><strong>4. Typography:</strong> Font names, sizes, weights, pairings. With real examples of headline + body.<br><strong>5. Photography Style Guide:</strong> The locked prompt template. Examples of correct and incorrect images.<br><strong>6. Voice & Tone:</strong> 3 adjectives. Examples of copy that sounds like the brand and copy that doesn\'t.<br><strong>7. Social Media Templates:</strong> Figma templates for each content pillar.<br><strong>8. Application Examples:</strong> Business card, Instagram post, email header — the brand in context.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#2563eb22;color:#2563eb">🎯</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">How to Present Brand Work to a Client</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The presentation is part of the deliverable</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Most designers let the work speak for itself — which is a mistake. Clients are not trained to read design. They see colors and think "I\'m not sure about the green." A professional presentation guides the client through the strategic reasoning <strong>before</strong> showing the visual. When they understand why, they rarely reject the what.</p><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Lead with Strategy, Not Aesthetics</div><div class="step-desc">Begin with: "Here is who your audience is, here is the emotional response we need to create, here is the competitive landscape we are designing against." Then show the work as the logical conclusion of that strategy.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Show Context, Not Just Executions</div><div class="step-desc">Present the logo on a business card, the colors on a website mockup, the typography in a social media post. Never show a logo floating on white paper — that is not how a client will ever see it.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Present 2 Options, Not 1, Not 5</div><div class="step-desc">1 option = "take it or leave it" (creates resistance). 5 options = paralysis. 2 clearly differentiated directions lets the client feel agency while guiding them toward the better option.</div></div></li></ul></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon green">📋</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">What a Complete Brand Package Actually Contains</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">The 7 deliverables clients expect — and pay for</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Brand Strategy One-Pager</div><div class="step-desc">Who the brand is. Who they serve. What they believe. What makes them different. This is 1 page — not a 40-slide deck. Strategy drives every creative decision that follows.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Logo System (3 versions minimum)</div><div class="step-desc">Primary logo. Secondary/compact version. Monogram or icon. All in vector. All in black, white, and brand color. All tested at small sizes.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Color System</div><div class="step-desc">Primary palette (1-2 colors). Secondary palette (2-3 supporting). Neutral system. All with hex, RGB, and CMYK values. Usage rules: which color is used where.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Typography System</div><div class="step-desc">Headline font. Body font. Optional accent font. Hierarchy example (H1, H2, H3, body, caption). Web-safe alternatives for clients who need them.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Photography Style Guide</div><div class="step-desc">3-5 approved images that define the visual language. Written rules: lighting, composition, color treatment, what to avoid. This is what you built in Day 31.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">6</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Usage Examples</div><div class="step-desc">Brand applied to 2-3 real touchpoints: social post mockup, business card, email header, or packaging. Shows the client what their brand looks like in the world.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">7</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Brand Guidelines PDF</div><div class="step-desc">The master document combining all of the above. Usually 12-25 pages. Figma template works perfectly. This is what they give their next designer or printer.</div></div></li> </ul> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 38: Complete Brand Package — Phase 4 Final',emoji:'📋',desc:'Assemble all Phase 4 work into a complete brand package for one brand. This is your Phase 4 portfolio piece — what you\'d deliver to a client.',deliverables:['The brand (can be from any Phase 4 day)','Package overview: what\'s included (logo, colors, type, photography, motion)','Your 3 strongest assets from Phase 4 — describe each','What would you add or improve before showing this to a real client?'],placeholder:'Brand:\n\nPackage includes:\n\nStrongest asset 1:\nStrongest asset 2:\nStrongest asset 3:\n\nWhat I\'d add before client delivery:\nMy biggest Phase 4 learning:',systemPrompt:'You are a brand design director reviewing a Phase 4 final presentation. Evaluate: does this package cover the essential brand system components? Are the assets described at a professional level of specificity? What\'s the weakest link that would undermine client confidence? Max 230 words.'}
};

