LESSONS[39]={
  phase:'Phase 5 — Portfolio Launch',title:'Day 39: Portfolio Strategy',
  tools:['Framer','Figma'],time:'60 min',tags:['portfolio','strategy','positioning','career'],
  videos:[
    {title:'AI Designer Portfolio Strategy — What Clients Actually Want',channel:'The Futur',duration:'36:20',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AI+designer+portfolio+strategy+clients+want+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#2c3e50,#4ca1af)'},
    {title:'Portfolio Mistakes to Avoid — What Kills Your Chances',channel:'Sagi Haviv',duration:'24:10',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=portfolio+mistakes+designer+avoid+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1a1a,#6c6c6c)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Awwwards — Best Portfolio Sites',desc:'Award-winning portfolio websites. Study structure, case study depth, and how designers present their process.',type:'website',url:'https://www.awwwards.com/websites/portfolio/',tag:'Benchmark'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#ef444422;color:#ef4444">🚀</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">A Portfolio is Not a Gallery — It\'s a Sales Document</div><div class="concept-card-sub">Every decision should answer: does this make a client want to hire me?</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Most designers build portfolios to impress other designers. This is a strategic mistake. Your portfolio audience is clients — people who want problems solved and results delivered. They do not evaluate your work on aesthetic merit. They evaluate it on one question: <strong>"Can this person solve my problem?"</strong> Your portfolio must answer that question in the first 10 seconds.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#fee2e2;border-color:#ef4444"><span class="hb-icon">🎯</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The 3 Portfolio Positioning Decisions</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>Decision 1 — Specialist vs Generalist:</strong> Specialists get paid more. "I create AI video ads for D2C beauty brands" charges 3x more than "I do AI design work." Pick a niche wide enough to have clients, narrow enough to be the obvious choice.<br><strong>Decision 2 — Process vs Result:</strong> Show both. Results prove you can deliver. Process proves you can think. A case study without process looks like luck. Process without results looks like theory.<br><strong>Decision 3 — Volume vs Depth:</strong> 4 deep case studies beat 20 shallow projects every time. Depth demonstrates mastery. Volume suggests you\'re a generalist who does quick work.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#f59e0b22;color:#f59e0b">📐</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">What Goes in Each Portfolio Project</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The anatomy of a case study that converts</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#ef4444">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">The Brief (1 sentence)</div><div class="step-desc">"A premium skincare brand needed a complete visual identity system for their UK launch." Client context + problem + scope. Never start with what you did — start with what the client needed.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#ef4444">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">The Strategy (2–3 sentences)</div><div class="step-desc">Your thinking before the making. "The competitor landscape was dominated by clinical whites and blues. We identified white space in the warm, human, artisan segment. All visual decisions flowed from this positioning."</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#ef4444">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">The Work (6–8 images)</div><div class="step-desc">Show the range of the system: logo, typography, color in use, photography examples, social media templates, packaging if relevant. Every image should be in-context, not floating on white.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#ef4444">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">The Result (quantified if possible)</div><div class="st-desc">"Instagram engagement increased 340% in 3 months." Or: "Brand launched successfully in 3 UK retailers." If you can\'t quantify, qualify: "Client described the rebrand as the best creative investment they made in 2024."</div></div></li></ul></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon amber">🎯</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">The Niche Selection Framework</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">Why "I do everything" gets you nothing — and how to pick your lane</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <p>The biggest mistake new AI designers make: building a portfolio that shows everything. A brand photo, a video ad, a poster, a logo — all for different industries. This signals "I am a generalist" which means "I am not the expert for your specific problem."</p> <div class="highlight-box amber"> <span class="hb-icon">💡</span> <div class="hb-content"> <div class="hb-title">The Counter-Intuitive Truth About Niching</div> <div class="hb-text">Niching does not limit your clients — it clarifies to the right clients that you are exactly right for them. A beauty brand founder who sees 3 portfolios — one generalist, one "brand + social," one "beauty + wellness brand photography" — will contact the third one every time. Because it already feels like you know their world.</div> </div> </div> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">A</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Industry Niche</div><div class="step-desc">Pick one: beauty + wellness / food + hospitality / tech + SaaS / fashion + lifestyle / healthcare / fintech. Deep industry knowledge creates trust. You know the visual language, the competitors, the buyer problems.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">B</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Service Niche</div><div class="step-desc">Pick one: brand photography system / social content production / video ad campaigns / complete brand identity. One clear service means clear pricing, clear process, clear client expectations.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">C</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">The Formula</div><div class="step-desc">"I create [service] for [industry] brands to [specific outcome]." Example: "I create AI-powered brand photography systems for wellness brands to maintain visual consistency across all marketing channels." This is 10 times more compelling than "I do AI design for brands."</div></div></li> </ul> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 39: Define Your Portfolio Positioning',emoji:'🚀',desc:'Get your positioning right before building anything. Most designers fail at this first step. Define your specialist angle, niche, and the 4 projects that will open the right doors.',deliverables:['Your specialist statement: \'I create [what] for [who] to achieve [outcome]\'','Your niche: which industry or style are you going deepest on?','4 portfolio projects you\'ll feature — name + one-line description each','What makes your portfolio different from 1,000 other AI designers?'],placeholder:'Specialist statement:\n\nNiche:\n\nProject 1:\nProject 2:\nProject 3:\nProject 4:\n\nMy differentiator:',systemPrompt:'You are a portfolio strategist and creative career coach. Review the student\'s positioning. Is the specialist statement specific or generic? Does their niche choice create real market differentiation? Give them honest feedback on whether their differentiator is actually compelling to a buyer. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[40]={
  phase:'Phase 5 — Portfolio Launch',title:'Day 40: Case Study Writing',
  tools:['Figma','Gemini'],time:'55 min',tags:['writing','case study','storytelling','portfolio'],
  videos:[
    {title:'How to Write a Design Case Study — Portfolio Writing',channel:'The Futur',duration:'33:40',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how+to+write+design+case+study+portfolio+writing+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#2c3e50,#4ca1af)'},
    {title:'Case Study Examples That Get Designers Hired',channel:'Sagi Haviv',duration:'22:30',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=case+study+portfolio+designer+hired+examples+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1a1a,#6c6c6c)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Medium Design Case Studies',desc:'Real designer case studies. Read 5 of the best-performing ones and note the narrative structure they share.',type:'website',url:'https://medium.com/tag/design-case-study',tag:'Reference'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#ef444422;color:#ef4444">✍️</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Writing Converts Better Than Showing</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The designer who can write earns 2x the designer who can only show</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Two designers submit equal portfolios. One has 6 images. One has 6 images and a 200-word case study for each. The second designer gets the client. Why? Because writing proves you understand the problem, not just the execution. Writing is the signal that separates a skilled executor from a strategic thinker. <strong>Every senior creative role, every agency client, and every high-value freelance project requires the ability to articulate your thinking in words.</strong></p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#fee2e2;border-color:#ef4444"><span class="hb-icon">📝</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The 5-Sentence Case Study Formula</div><div class="hb-text">This is the minimum viable case study. Master this before going longer.<br><br><strong>Sentence 1 — The Client:</strong> "Bloom is a D2C skincare brand founded in Mumbai targeting urban professional women 27–38."<br><strong>Sentence 2 — The Problem:</strong> "They had a great product but looked indistinguishable from the 50 other minimal-white skincare brands in their category."<br><strong>Sentence 3 — The Insight:</strong> "We identified that their authentic founder story was their most differentiating asset — something no competitor was using visually."<br><strong>Sentence 4 — The Solution:</strong> "We built a visual identity system around warmth, origin, and human texture — replacing clinical whites with aged terracotta, cream, and gold."<br><strong>Sentence 5 — The Result:</strong> "Within 6 months of launch, Bloom achieved 28,000 Instagram followers and secured distribution in 12 premium Nykaa stores."</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#2563eb22;color:#2563eb">🤖</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Using Claude to Write Case Studies Faster</div><div class="concept-card-sub">AI-assisted writing without losing your voice</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Claude can help you write case studies — but only if you provide the raw material. The formula: give Claude the brief, the insight, the solution, and the result as bullet points. Ask it to write in your specified tone. Then edit to add your voice. Never ask Claude to "write my case study" from nothing — it will produce generic content that sounds like everyone else\'s case study.</p><div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-card-head"><span class="prompt-card-label">// CLAUDE CASE STUDY PROMPT</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt(this,\'Write a 150-word portfolio case study for an AI brand designer. Use this raw material:\nClient: [name + description]\nProblem: [exact problem]\nInsight: [strategic insight]\nSolution: [what was created]\nResult: [measurable outcome]\n\nTone: confident but not arrogant, strategic, specific. Start with the client and problem. Do not use buzzwords like innovative, cutting-edge, or transformative. Write in present tense. End with the result.\')">📋 Copy</button></div><div class="prompt-card-body">Write a 150-word portfolio case study. Tone: confident, strategic, specific. No buzzwords. Start with the client problem. End with the result. [Provide your raw material as bullet points below this prompt]</div></div></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon green">✍️</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">The 5-Sentence Case Study Formula</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">Every award-winning portfolio case study follows this exact structure</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">The Client + The Problem</div><div class="step-desc">"[Client] is a [describe in 5 words]. They needed [specific problem — not better visuals but a visual identity that could compete with established luxury brands at 40% of their budget]."</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Your Strategic Insight</div><div class="step-desc">"After reviewing their market, I identified that [insight that drove your creative direction]. This insight shaped every creative decision."</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">The Work You Did</div><div class="step-desc">"I created [specific deliverables] using [tools/approach]. The key creative decision was [one thing that made this project distinct]."</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">The Specific Result</div><div class="step-desc">"The campaign resulted in [specific metric: engagement rate, follower growth, conversion rate]." If no metrics: "The client used the system to produce 60 pieces of content in the first month, compared to 8 per month previously."</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">What This Demonstrates</div><div class="step-desc">"This project demonstrates my ability to [skill statement connecting to your positioning]." This sentence connects the case study to why the next client should hire you.</div></div></li> </ul> <div class="highlight-box blue"> <span class="hb-icon">⚠️</span> <div class="hb-content"> <div class="hb-title">The Mistake That Kills Portfolio Conversion</div> <div class="hb-text">Most designers end case studies with "the client was happy." This tells the next client nothing. Happiness is the minimum bar. Always end with a specific outcome — numbers, behavior change, business impact, or a direct client quote with specifics.</div> </div> </div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon green">🔧</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">Tool Spotlight: Gemini for Professional Writing</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">Free AI for case studies, proposals, and client communications</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <p><strong>Gemini</strong> (gemini.google.com) — Free. Clean professional prose. Prompt: "Write a 150-word case study: Client + Problem, Insight, Work, Result." Integrates with Google Docs. <a href="https://gemini.google.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--primary);font-weight:700;">Open Gemini →</a></p> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 40: Write 1 Case Study — Complete',emoji:'✍️',desc:'Write a full case study for your strongest portfolio project. The 5-sentence formula is the skeleton — now add the flesh. This is what converts a viewer into a client.',deliverables:['Project chosen + original brief (what the client needed)','Your process: 3 key decisions you made and why','The result: specific, visual, outcome-focused (not \'the client was happy\')','Full case study draft — max 200 words'],placeholder:'Project:\nOriginal brief:\n\nDecision 1 + why:\nDecision 2 + why:\nDecision 3 + why:\n\nResult (specific):\n\nFull case study (max 200 words):',systemPrompt:'You are a portfolio writing specialist. Review the student\'s case study. Does it follow the problem → process → result arc? Is the result specific and outcome-focused? Give them line-level feedback on the weakest sentence and rewrite it as a model. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[41]={
  phase:'Phase 5 — Portfolio Launch',title:'Day 41: Portfolio Website with Framer',
  tools:['Framer','Figma'],time:'75 min',tags:['framer','website','portfolio','launch'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Framer Portfolio Website — Complete Build in 2025',channel:'Theoretically Media',duration:'36:20',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=framer+portfolio+website+complete+build+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#667eea,#764ba2)'},
    {title:'AI Portfolio Site — Framer + AI Tools Workflow',channel:'Matt Wolfe',duration:'28:40',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AI+designer+portfolio+framer+website+launch+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#f7971e,#ffd200)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Framer Template Gallery',desc:'The highest quality Framer portfolio templates. Choose one, adapt it — don\'t build from scratch.',type:'website',url:'https://www.framer.com/templates/',tag:'Tool'},
    {name:'Awwwards Portfolio Sites',desc:'Award-winning designer portfolios. Study information architecture and first impression.',type:'website',url:'https://www.awwwards.com/websites/portfolio/',tag:'Benchmark'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#ef444422;color:#ef4444">🌐</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Framer — The AI Designer\'s Best Portfolio Tool</div><div class="concept-card-sub">AI-generated layouts, no-code publishing, instant domain</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Framer is the leading no-code website builder for designers. It differs from Squarespace or Wix in one critical way: it produces pixel-perfect, animation-rich websites that look like they were hand-coded by a senior frontend developer. For an AI designer showcasing AI work, the portfolio site itself should signal technical and aesthetic sophistication.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#fee2e2;border-color:#ef4444"><span class="hb-icon">🎯</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">Your Portfolio Site Must-Haves</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>Above the fold:</strong> Your name, your one-line positioning statement, and your best project — visible without scrolling. 10 seconds to make an impression.<br><strong>Navigation:</strong> Max 4 links. Work / About / Services or Process / Contact. Anything else is dilution.<br><strong>Project pages:</strong> Each project gets a dedicated page. The thumbnail is the hook — make it cinematic.<br><strong>About page:</strong> Photo (real, warm, professional), 2-paragraph bio (who you are, who you help, what makes you different), and a clear CTA ("View my work" or "Start a project").<br><strong>Contact:</strong> One form, one email. Never make a client search for how to reach you.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#10b98122;color:#10b981">🚀</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Launch Checklist — What to Have Before Going Live</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The 15 things that make the difference between amateur and professional</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">✓</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Content Checklist</div><div class="step-desc">4 case studies minimum · Each with Brief/Strategy/Work/Result · Hero image for each project that works as a scroll-stopping thumbnail · 2-paragraph About bio · Services with pricing or "pricing on request"</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">✓</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Technical Checklist</div><div class="st-desc">Custom domain (not .framer.app — it signals you\'re not serious) · Mobile-optimized (check on your phone) · Page speed under 3 seconds · All images optimized (compress before upload) · Contact form tested</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#10b981">✓</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Brand Checklist</div><div class="st-desc">Site color palette matches your brand · Typography is consistent · Favicon set · Social preview image set (what appears when you share the link on LinkedIn)</div></div></li></ul></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon blue">🌐</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">The 5 Elements of a Portfolio That Converts</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">Most portfolios look good. These 5 make them actually get you clients.</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">A Positioning Statement Above the Fold</div><div class="step-desc">Your hero section must answer in 3 seconds: who you are, what you do, who for. Not "creative designer" — but "I create AI brand photography systems for wellness and beauty brands." The visitor immediately knows if you are relevant to them.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">3-5 Projects Maximum</div><div class="step-desc">Never show everything. Show your 3-5 best, most relevant projects. Quality creates more trust than volume. A visitor who sees 15 mediocre projects remembers nothing. A visitor who sees 4 exceptional case studies remembers you.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">One Clear CTA Everywhere</div><div class="step-desc">Every section should point toward the same action: book a call, send an email, or view your services. One CTA repeated is better than 4 different options. "Work with me" — not "Work with me / View pricing / See services / Contact."</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">A Process Section</div><div class="step-desc">Clients hire designers they trust to handle the process. A 3-4 step process section (Discovery, Strategy, Creation, Delivery) shows you have a professional system. It reduces the perceived risk of working with you.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Social Proof — Even 1 Testimonial</div><div class="step-desc">One specific client testimonial outperforms zero. Add it as soon as you have your first real client interaction. Even a brief honest note from a real person converts better than a polished page with no social proof.</div></div></li> </ul> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 41: Launch Your Portfolio Site',emoji:'🌐',desc:'Build and publish your Framer portfolio. By end of this session, your site should be live. Choose, customize, and ship — no perfection paralysis.',deliverables:['Framer template chosen + why it fits your brand personality','3 customizations made: colors, fonts, and hero copy','Your live site URL (or describe your progress if not live yet)','What\'s the one thing you\'d improve in the next iteration?'],placeholder:'Template chosen:\nWhy it fits my brand:\n\nCustomization 1 (colors):\nCustomization 2 (fonts):\nCustomization 3 (hero copy):\n\nSite URL or progress update:\n\nNext iteration improvement:',systemPrompt:'You are a portfolio design director and Framer specialist. Review the student\'s site customization choices. Do the three customizations create a coherent brand identity? Is the hero copy compelling to a potential client? Give specific feedback on what would make the first impression stronger. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[42]={
  phase:'Phase 5 — Portfolio Launch',title:'Day 42: Pricing Your AI Design Services',
  tools:['Figma','Gemini'],time:'55 min',tags:['pricing','business','freelance','services'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Pricing Creative Work — The Definitive Guide',channel:'The Futur',duration:'42:10',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pricing+creative+work+freelance+designer+definitive+guide',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#2c3e50,#4ca1af)'},
    {title:'AI Designer Rates — What to Charge in 2025',channel:'Matt Wolfe',duration:'19:30',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AI+designer+rates+what+to+charge+2026+freelance',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#f7971e,#ffd200)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'The Futur — Pricing Guide',desc:'The most respected resource on creative pricing. Chris Do\'s pricing framework has helped thousands of designers.',type:'website',url:'https://thefutur.com/content/how-to-price-your-design-work',tag:'Essential'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#ef444422;color:#ef4444">💰</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Price on Value, Never on Hours</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The single pricing insight that changes a freelance career</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Hourly pricing is a trap. When you charge by the hour, two things happen: (1) you are rewarded for being slow — taking longer earns more, (2) clients focus on how many hours you take rather than what you deliver. Value-based pricing asks one question: <strong>what is this work worth to the client?</strong> A logo that helps launch a brand that generates £500,000 in year one is worth far more than 10 hours at £50/hour.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#fee2e2;border-color:#ef4444"><span class="hb-icon">📐</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The AI Designer Service Pricing Framework (2025)</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>Individual AI Images (social, ads):</strong> £50–200 per final image. Volume packages: 20 images/month retainer £600–1,200.<br><strong>Brand Identity Package (logo + guidelines + templates):</strong> £1,500–5,000. Depends on business size and scope.<br><strong>AI Video Ad (15–30 seconds):</strong> £300–800 per video. 3-video package: £750–2,000.<br><strong>Complete Brand Content System (strategy + identity + 3 months content):</strong> £3,000–10,000.<br><strong>Monthly Retainer (content production, 30 posts + 4 videos):</strong> £1,500–4,000/month.<br><br>These are UK/international market rates as of 2025. Adjust for your local market.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#f59e0b22;color:#f59e0b">📋</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">The 3 Service Packages — Good, Better, Best</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The structure that makes pricing easy for clients and profitable for you</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>When a client asks "how much?" the worst answer is a custom quote. The best answer is a menu of 3 packages. Research shows that when given 3 options, 60% of buyers choose the middle option. Design your packages so the middle option is your preferred scope and margin.</p><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#ef4444">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Starter Package (entry price point)</div><div class="st-desc">Scoped to deliver real value at a price that removes purchase risk. Example: "Brand Identity Starter — logo, color palette, typography guide, 5 social templates — £1,200." Designed to bring new clients in at low risk, then upsell to ongoing retainer.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#ef4444">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Pro Package (your target sale)</div><div class="st-desc">Full scope, full value. "Complete Brand System — everything in Starter + photography style guide, 2 video ads, 1 month content — £3,500." This is what you want to sell most of the time. Priced to be genuinely great value for the client while being profitable for you.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#ef4444">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Premium Package (anchor price)</div><div class="st-desc">"Full Brand Partnership — everything in Pro + monthly content retainer, quarterly brand review, priority turnaround — £6,500 + £2,000/month." Makes the Pro look affordable by comparison. Even if no one buys Premium, it improves Pro conversion by 40%.</div></div></li></ul></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon amber">💰</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">Why Hourly Pricing Kills Your Business</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">And how to think about value-based pricing as an AI designer</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <p>The problem with hourly pricing: as you get better and faster (which AI makes you dramatically), you earn less. A 30-image campaign that took a traditional photographer 3 days takes you 4 hours with AI. If you charge hourly, you cut your income by 90%.</p> <div class="highlight-box amber"> <span class="hb-icon">💡</span> <div class="hb-content"> <div class="hb-title">Value-Based Pricing Reality Check</div> <div class="hb-text">A brand photography campaign for a product launch is worth 50,000-2,00,000 rupees to the client — it appears in their ads, packaging, and social channels for 12+ months. Your cost: AI tools plus your expertise plus 4 hours. Price based on the value to the client, not your cost of production. Clients do not pay for hours — they pay for outcomes.</div> </div> </div> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">🟢</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Starter Package: 8,000-15,000 rupees</div><div class="step-desc">3 AI campaign images + 1 revision. For early-stage brands with limited budget who need professional imagery fast. Position as: "Launch-ready visuals in 72 hours."</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">🔵</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Pro Package: 25,000-50,000 rupees</div><div class="step-desc">10-image brand campaign system + style guide + 2 revisions. For growing brands who need consistent visual identity. Position as: "Your complete visual content system for one quarter."</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">⭐</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Premium Retainer: 40,000-80,000 rupees/month</div><div class="step-desc">Monthly brand photography + social content + ongoing style management. For established brands who need continuous content. Position as: "Your on-demand AI brand studio."</div></div></li> </ul> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 42: Design Your 3 Service Packages',emoji:'💰',desc:'Build your pricing structure: 3 tiered packages with real scope, deliverables, and prices. This is the foundation of your freelance business.',deliverables:['Starter package: scope, deliverables, price, turnaround','Pro package: scope, deliverables, price, turnaround','Premium/Retainer: scope, deliverables, price, turnaround','Which package do you think most clients will choose — and why?'],placeholder:'Starter:\nScope:\nDeliverables:\nPrice: ₹\nTurnaround:\n\nPro:\nScope:\nDeliverables:\nPrice: ₹\nTurnaround:\n\nPremium/Retainer:\nScope:\nDeliverables:\nPrice: ₹\nTurnaround:\n\nMost clients will pick [package] because:',systemPrompt:'You are a freelance business strategist. Review the student\'s 3 packages. Is there clear value escalation from Starter to Premium? Are the prices aligned with the Indian market while remaining profitable? Flag any scope that sounds unprofitable at the stated price. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[43]={
  phase:'Phase 5 — Portfolio Launch',title:'Day 43: Client Proposal Writing',
  tools:['Gemini','Gamma'],time:'60 min',tags:['proposals','client','writing','business'],
  videos:[
    {title:'How to Write a Creative Proposal — Win Clients',channel:'The Futur',duration:'36:40',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how+to+write+creative+proposal+win+clients+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#2c3e50,#4ca1af)'},
    {title:'Client Discovery Call — The 5 Questions That Win Projects',channel:'Sagi Haviv',duration:'24:20',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=client+discovery+call+questions+win+project+design+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1a1a,#6c6c6c)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'And.co Proposal Templates',desc:'Professional freelance proposal templates used by thousands of top-earning creatives.',type:'website',url:'https://www.and.co/guides/proposal-templates',tag:'Tool'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#ef444422;color:#ef4444">📄</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">A Proposal is Not a Quote — It\'s a Conversion Tool</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The job is to make the client feel understood, not impressed</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Most designers send price lists when asked for a proposal. This is why most designers compete on price. A professional proposal does something different: it <strong>re-articulates the client\'s problem in the client\'s language</strong>, demonstrates that you understand what they\'re trying to achieve, and then presents your solution as the logical, specific answer. When a client reads their own problem written back to them more clearly than they expressed it, they immediately feel you are the right person for the job — before they see the price.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#fee2e2;border-color:#ef4444"><span class="hb-icon">📐</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The 6-Section Proposal Structure</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>1. Understanding (1 paragraph):</strong> Re-articulate the client\'s problem in their language, showing you listened.<br><strong>2. Approach (1 paragraph):</strong> Your specific strategic thinking for this client — not a generic process description.<br><strong>3. Scope (bullet list):</strong> Exactly what is included. And what is not included (this is critical — it prevents scope creep).<br><strong>4. Timeline:</strong> Milestones with dates. Shows you\'ve actually thought through the project, not just quoted it.<br><strong>5. Investment:</strong> The price — presented as a total, with payment terms. Never send an hourly rate in a proposal.<br><strong>6. Next steps:</strong> One clear action to accept. "Reply with approval" or "Sign the attached contract."</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#2563eb22;color:#2563eb">💬</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">The Discovery Call — 5 Questions That Win Projects</div><div class="concept-card-sub">You cannot write a good proposal without first asking the right questions</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Never write a proposal without a discovery call. A proposal written without a call is a generic price list. A proposal written after a 30-minute discovery call is a specific, personal document that closes at 5x the rate.</p><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#2563eb">Q1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">"What does success look like for this project in 12 months?"</div><div class="st-desc">This reveals the real goal behind the brief. Clients rarely tell you their actual success metric in the brief. If they say "I want more Instagram followers" — that is a marketing problem. If they say "I want to raise our Series A" — that is a credibility problem. Different problems need different solutions.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#2563eb">Q2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">"What has stopped you from solving this before?"</div><div class="st-desc">Reveals whether there are internal constraints you need to design around (stakeholder politics, budget history, previous agency failures). Also shows you take problems seriously, not just executions.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#2563eb">Q3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">"Who else will be involved in decisions?"</div><div class="st-desc">Reveals the approval chain. If there are 5 stakeholders, you need to design your proposal to be compelling even when read by someone who wasn\'t on the call. Ask this every time.</div></div></li></ul></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon green">📄</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">The 6-Section Winning Proposal</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">What every section does — and why clients say yes or no</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">The Situation (2-3 sentences)</div><div class="step-desc">Prove you understood the brief by articulating their problem better than they did. "You have a loyal local following, but your visual identity does not reflect the quality of your product. You are losing customers to competitors with stronger visual brands, not better coffee."</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Your Approach (3-4 sentences)</div><div class="step-desc">Not "I will create beautiful images." Instead: "I will build a photography system with locked visual variables — your palette, lighting, and composition will be consistent across every image, giving you 60 months of consistent brand imagery from one production session."</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Scope of Work (bullet list)</div><div class="step-desc">Specific and countable. "15 AI campaign images. 1 brand photography style guide. 3 Figma social templates. 2 revision rounds." No vague language — vague scope causes client disputes and unpaid revision spirals.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Timeline</div><div class="step-desc">Week 1: Discovery and style guide. Week 2: First image batch and feedback. Week 3: Revisions and final delivery. Clients want to know when they will see results — give them a clear schedule.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Investment</div><div class="step-desc">State the price clearly. Then immediately follow with what it includes. "Investment: 35,000 rupees — includes 15 campaign images, brand style guide, 3 social templates, and 2 revision rounds." Never list price without the value.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">6</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Why You (2 sentences max)</div><div class="step-desc">Most designers write 3 paragraphs. Write 2 sentences. One specific differentiator + one result. "I specialize in AI-powered brand photography for food and hospitality brands. My last 3 restaurant clients increased social engagement by 40-60% in the first month."</div></div></li> </ul> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon green">🔧</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">Tool Spotlight: Gamma for Client Proposals</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">Turn proposal text into a polished presentation in 2 minutes</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <p><strong>Gamma</strong> (gamma.app) — Paste your proposal → polished slide deck instantly. Free tier: 10 presentations. <em>Workflow: Write in Gemini → paste into Gamma → customize → export PDF.</em> <a href="https://gamma.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--primary);font-weight:700;">Open Gamma →</a></p> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 43: Write a Proposal for the Brief Below',emoji:'📄',desc:'Write a focused proposal for a real client brief. Keep it sharp — clients don\'t read long proposals. Every section must earn its place.',deliverables:['Brief: \'Organic coffee brand, 3 years old, amateur visual identity, needs professional AI brand photography for social and packaging.\'','Your proposed scope (what exactly you will deliver)','Your timeline + investment (use your Day 42 pricing)','One sentence that answers: why you, and not anyone else?'],placeholder:'Proposed scope:\n\nTimeline:\nInvestment: ₹\n\nWhy me (one sentence):\n\nFull proposal draft (max 150 words):',systemPrompt:'You are a client proposal specialist. Review the student\'s proposal. Is the scope specific enough to execute and invoice against? Does the \'why me\' sentence actually differentiate them? Give honest feedback on whether this proposal would win the brief. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[44]={
  phase:'Phase 5 — Portfolio Launch',title:'Day 44: Your First Client Project',
  tools:['Midjourney v8','Kling 2.6','Figma'],time:'90 min',tags:['client','real world','delivery','process'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Managing Your First Design Client — Everything You Need to Know',channel:'The Futur',duration:'38:10',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=managing+first+design+client+everything+need+to+know+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#2c3e50,#4ca1af)'},
    {title:'Client Feedback Handling — How to Not Lose Your Mind',channel:'Sagi Haviv',duration:'26:40',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=client+feedback+design+how+to+handle+professionally+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1a1a,#6c6c6c)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Working Not Working — Creative Rates',desc:'Real freelance creative rates and client project structures from top-tier creative professionals.',type:'website',url:'https://workingnotworking.com',tag:'Reference'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#ef444422;color:#ef4444">🤝</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Client Management is 50% of the Job</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The work gets you hired. How you manage gets you rehired.</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>Most designers focus 95% of their energy on the quality of the work and 5% on the quality of the client relationship. Experienced freelancers know the ratio is closer to 50/50. A great designer with poor client management loses clients. A good designer with excellent client management builds a waiting list. <strong>The client experience is the product.</strong></p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#fee2e2;border-color:#ef4444"><span class="hb-icon">⚙️</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The Client Project Lifecycle — 5 Phases</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>Phase 1 — Onboarding:</strong> Contract signed, deposit paid (50% upfront — always), project brief confirmed in writing, timeline shared.<br><strong>Phase 2 — Discovery:</strong> Brand questionnaire, competitor audit, mood board presentation. Get alignment on direction BEFORE creating anything.<br><strong>Phase 3 — Concept presentation:</strong> 2 directions (not 1, not 5). Strategy first, aesthetic second. Invite feedback on strategy before aesthetics.<br><strong>Phase 4 — Refinement:</strong> Defined revision rounds (2 included — state this in contract). Changes outside scope are charged as additions.<br><strong>Phase 5 — Delivery:</strong> Final files in agreed formats. Brand guidelines PDF. "How to use this" video walkthrough. Request testimonial 2 weeks after delivery.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#f59e0b22;color:#f59e0b">💬</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Handling Difficult Feedback Without Losing the Project</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The 3 responses that keep the project on track</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>"I\'m just not feeling it." "My wife thinks it looks too dark." "Can we make it pop more?" These are the feedback lines that send junior designers into a panic. Professional designers have learned that vague feedback is always a proxy for a specific underlying concern — and the job is to find the concern, not react to the surface comment.</p><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#f59e0b">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">The Clarifying Question</div><div class="st-desc">Never react to vague feedback. Always ask: "When you say [feedback], can you tell me more about what specifically isn\'t working for you? Is it the color, the weight, the layout, or something else?" This turns a vague rejection into an actionable direction.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#f59e0b">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">The Strategic Anchor</div><div class="st-desc">"That\'s useful feedback. I want to make sure any changes still serve the strategic goal we agreed on, which was [X]. Can we discuss whether this change moves us toward or away from that?" This redirects from taste to strategy, where you are on stronger ground.</div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#f59e0b">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">The "Yes and" Accommodation</div><div class="st-desc">If the feedback is genuinely a preference, not a strategic issue: "I can make that change. Before I do, can I show you a quick comparison so we can evaluate both together?" This maintains your authority while making the client feel heard.</div></div></li></ul></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon blue">🤝</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">The 5-Phase Client Project System</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">Run every client project through this and you will never have chaos</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Onboarding (Day 1)</div><div class="step-desc">Send a short onboarding form — 5 questions max. What do you want to achieve? Who is your target customer? Which brands do you admire visually? Any visual restrictions? What does success look like in 3 months? Get answers before any creative work begins.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Strategy (Days 1-3)</div><div class="step-desc">Build a mood board. Write the visual brief. Define locked variables. Get ONE approval on direction before generating any final images. "Do you approve this visual direction?" is the most important question you will ask — it protects you from revision spirals.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Execution (Days 3-7)</div><div class="step-desc">Generate images using the approved brief. Select 2x what you will deliver. Curate down to the contracted amount. Never show the AI raw output — always curate first. The curation is the skill the client is paying for.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Feedback (Days 7-10)</div><div class="step-desc">Present work in context — mock it up in their social feed or on their product. Ask: "Which 3 images do you love most? What specifically works in those?" Vague feedback means you did not get direction approval right — loop back to Phase 2.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Delivery + Upsell (Days 10-14)</div><div class="step-desc">Deliver all final files in agreed formats with a brief usage guide. Then 48 hours after delivery, send a value review: "Here is what we accomplished. Here is what we could do next quarter to build on this." The upsell is not a sale — it is a continuation of service.</div></div></li> </ul> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 44: Run Your First Client Project',emoji:'🤝',desc:'Simulate a full client project lifecycle using the Phase 3 brief. Move through all 5 phases in sequence. This is your first professional run-through.',deliverables:['Brief: \'Wellness supplement brand needs a 3-image campaign for Instagram launch.\'','Phase 1: Onboarding — your questions list (max 5 questions you\'d ask the client)','Phase 2: Concept — your visual brief in 50 words','Phase 3: Execution — 3 image prompts for the campaign'],placeholder:'Phase 1 — 5 onboarding questions:\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n5.\n\nPhase 2 — Visual brief (50 words):\n\nPhase 3 — Campaign prompts:\nImage 1:\nImage 2:\nImage 3:',systemPrompt:'You are a project director reviewing a student\'s first simulated client project. Evaluate: are the onboarding questions strategic or surface-level? Does the visual brief give clear direction? Do the 3 image prompts actually match the brief? Give feedback on where the process would break down with a real client. Max 230 words.'}
};

LESSONS[45]={
  phase:'Phase 5 — Portfolio Launch',title:'Day 45: Launch & Build Your 90-Second Showreel',
  tools:['Kling 2.6','CapCut Pro','ElevenLabs'],time:'90 min',tags:['showreel','launch','portfolio','career'],
  videos:[
    {title:'Design Showreel — How to Create a Portfolio Reel That Gets Work',channel:'The Futur',duration:'32:20',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=design+showreel+portfolio+reel+gets+work+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#2c3e50,#4ca1af)'},
    {title:'CapCut Pro Showreel Editing — Complete Workflow',channel:'Theoretically Media',duration:'28:40',url:'https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=capcut+pro+showreel+editing+complete+workflow+2026',bg:'linear-gradient(135deg,#f7971e,#ffd200)'},
  ],
  inspiration:[
    {name:'Motionographer — Design Reels',desc:'The best creative showreels in the industry. Study the pacing, music choice, and sequence logic.',type:'website',url:'https://motionographer.com',tag:'Benchmark'},
  ],
  imageRefs:[],
  content:'<div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#ef444422;color:#ef4444">🏆</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Day 45 — You\'ve Built Something Real</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The showreel is not the end. It\'s the beginning.</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><p>A showreel is the most powerful portfolio asset you can own. It takes everything you\'ve created — images, videos, brand systems, motion pieces — and assembles it into 90 seconds of evidence. Done well, a showreel communicates your range, your standard, and your aesthetic in less time than it takes to read a CV. <strong>It is the thing you send when someone asks "can you show me your work?"</strong></p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:#fee2e2;border-color:#ef4444"><span class="hb-icon">🎬</span><div class="hb-content"><div class="hb-title">The 90-Second Showreel Structure</div><div class="hb-text"><strong>0–3s — The Hook:</strong> Your most visually striking piece of work. No intro text, no your name. Just your best image or video. Earn the viewer\'s attention before asking for anything.<br><strong>3–30s — Range:</strong> 6–8 rapid cuts showing the breadth of your work. Images, video stills, brand systems, motion pieces. Each cut on the beat. Show that you can do more than one thing.<br><strong>30–70s — Depth:</strong> 3–4 longer holds (2–3 seconds each) on your strongest projects with project name lower-third text. This is where clients see quality, not just quantity.<br><strong>70–85s — Signature piece:</strong> Your absolute best work. Give it time to breathe. Let the viewer feel the quality.<br><strong>85–90s — Call to action:</strong> Your name + website URL. Simple, clean, confident. No music fade-out swell — let the last frame hold in silence for 1 beat.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="concept-card"><div class="concept-card-head"><div class="concept-icon" style="background:#10b98122;color:#10b981">🚀</div><div><div class="concept-card-title">Your Post-Launch Checklist — What Happens After Day 45</div><div class="concept-card-sub">The work you did in 45 days builds the foundation. What you do next builds the career.</div></div></div><div class="concept-card-body"><ul class="steps-list"><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#ef4444">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Week 1: Publish and Distribute</div><div class="st-desc">Post your showreel on LinkedIn (native video, not a link), Instagram Reels, and your portfolio site. Write a launch post that tells the story of what you built in 45 days. Tag the brands you created work for (even concept work). This post matters — it signals your entry into the market.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#ef4444">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Month 1: Outreach to 20 Potential Clients</div><div class="st-desc">Identify 20 brands in your niche whose visual identity could be improved. Send a personalised 3-sentence message: what you noticed, what you would change, a link to your work. Not a sales pitch — a conversation starter. 20 messages, 2–4 responses, 1–2 meetings is a normal conversion rate.</div></div></li><li class="step-item"><div class="step-num" style="background:#ef4444">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Month 2–3: Keep Creating</div><div class="st-desc">The designers who build careers in AI are the ones who continue making work even without client briefs. Create concept projects for brands you love. Post one piece of work per week. Your audience compounds. Your skills compound. Your prices compound. This is the only strategy that works.</div></div></li></ul></div></div> <div class="concept-card"> <div class="concept-card-head"> <div class="concept-icon purple" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#667eea,#764ba2)">🏆</div> <div> <div class="concept-card-title">The 90-Second Showreel Structure</div> <div class="concept-card-sub">Every second is intentional. Here is exactly how to use them.</div> </div> </div> <div class="concept-card-body"> <ul class="steps-list"> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">0-3s</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">The Hook — Your Most Striking Shot</div><div class="step-desc">No title card. No intro. Your strongest image or video clip, cold. The viewer decides to watch or not in 3 seconds. Lead with impact. If you have one piece that makes people say "wait, AI made that?" — that goes first.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3-30s</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Range Section — Show Your Breadth</div><div class="step-desc">6-8 clips, 3-4 seconds each, cut on the beat. Different styles, categories, moods. Fashion + food + tech + architecture = range. Fast-cut rhythm creates energy and shows confidence. This is the "I can handle your brief" section.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">30-70s</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Depth Section — Your 4 Best Projects</div><div class="step-desc">4 project sequences, 10 seconds each. Show 2-3 images from the same project to demonstrate consistency and craft. The pace slows here — you want viewers to actually look. This is the "I do this at professional level" section.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">70-85s</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Signature — Your Most Distinctive Work</div><div class="step-desc">One project, 15 seconds. This is your calling card — the work you want to be known for, the style you want more briefs in. If you want luxury clients, your signature is your best luxury work.</div></div></li> <li class="step-item"><div class="step-num">85-90s</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">CTA — Name + Service + Contact</div><div class="step-desc">Simple text overlay: your name, what you do, your contact (email or Instagram handle). Hold for 5 seconds. Music ends cleanly on a full beat. A confident stop — not a fade.</div></div></li> </ul> <div class="highlight-box green"> <span class="hb-icon">🚀</span> <div class="hb-content"> <div class="hb-title">Where to Publish First — and How</div> <div class="hb-text">LinkedIn first (highest organic reach for professional services). Write a launch post: what you built over 45 days, 1-2 surprising things you learned, what you now offer. Tag 3 people who inspired your work. Post the video natively — no YouTube link, LinkedIn suppresses links. Then Instagram Reels, then Twitter/X, then Behance as a full case study.</div> </div> </div> </div> </div>',
  assignment:{title:'Day 45: Build Your 90-Second Showreel',emoji:'🏆',desc:'This is the course capstone. Assemble your strongest work into a 90-second showreel, publish it, and write your launch post. This is how AI designers get their first clients.',deliverables:['Your 6 strongest pieces from the course — one line each on why they\'re strongest','Showreel structure: what goes first, what\'s the midpoint peak, what closes?','Your LinkedIn launch post (max 100 words — punchy, specific, you)','Where will you publish it first and why that platform?'],placeholder:'6 strongest pieces:\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n5.\n6.\n\nShowreel structure:\nOpening:\nMidpoint peak:\nClose:\n\nLinkedIn post (max 100 words):\n\nPublish platform first:\nWhy:',systemPrompt:'You are a creative director and portfolio launch strategist. Review the student\'s showreel plan. Does the structure build and peak correctly? Is the LinkedIn post specific and compelling or generic and humble? Give them one concrete edit that would make the post more likely to be shared. Max 230 words.'}
};



